0$,\n    59\t\twe write $\\deg_c(\\ell)$ for the set of univariate polynomials of degree\n    60\t\tat most $c$, each given by its list of $c+1$ coefficients and identified\n    61\t\tby its polynomial representative rather than by the function it induces,\n    62\t\tas in Definition~\\ref{def:polyfunc}; the argument $\\ell$ records the\n    63\t\tline to which such an answer refers, through the parameterization\n    64\t\t$t \\mapsto u_0 + tv$. The cases used below are $\\deg_d(\\ell)$ and\n    65\t\t$\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$; whenever $d \\leq md$, extending coefficient lists by\n    66\t\tzeros gives the inclusion $\\deg_d(\\ell) \\subseteq \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$. For\n    67\t\t$f \\in \\deg_c(\\ell)$, $u \\in \\ell$, and $a \\in \\F_q$, we say that $f$\n    68\t\t\\emph{evaluates to $a$ at $u$}, written $f(u) = a$, if $f(t) = a$ for\n    69\t\tevery $t \\in \\F_q$ with $u = u_0 + tv$. When $v \\neq 0$ this $t$ is\n    70\t\tunique, so every $f$ evaluates at every point of $\\ell$; when $v = 0$,\n    71\t\tso that $\\ell = \\{u_0\\}$, exactly those $f$ that induce a constant\n    72\t\tfunction on $\\F_q$ evaluate at $u_0$, to their constant value. An\n    73\t\toperator indexed by $f(u)$ is understood to be the zero operator when\n    74\t\t$f$ does not evaluate at $u$, and for a family $\\{N_f\\}$ indexed by\n    75\t\t$f \\in \\deg_c(\\ell)$ we write\n    76\t\t$N_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]} = \\sum_{f \\colon f(u) = a} N_f$; when\n    77\t\t$v = 0$ the classes indexed by $a \\in \\F_q$ form a sub-measurement\n    78\t\trather than a partition of the outcomes.\n    79\t\\end{definition}\n    80\t\n    81\t\\begin{remark}[Line answers as coefficient lists]\n    82\t\t\\label{rem:deg-line-representatives}\n    83\t\tThe source refers to the elements of $\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$ as polynomials\n    84\t\t``defined on the line $\\ell$'', that is, as functions on the point set\n    85\t\tof $\\ell$, while its decision procedure expects each line answer as a\n    86\t\tlist of coefficients. The two readings differ. When $md \\geq q$,\n    87\t\tdistinct coefficient lists induce the same function $\\F_q \\to \\F_q$;\n    88\t\tand when $\\ell$ has direction $v = 0$ --- a case the question\n    89\t\tdistributions produce with positive probability\n    90\t\t(Remark~\\ref{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}) --- the point set of $\\ell$ is\n    91\t\tthe singleton $\\{u_0\\}$, so a function on $\\ell$ retains only a single\n    92\t\tfield element of the answer. Definition~\\ref{def:ideg-deg-polynomials}\n    93\t\ttherefore adopts the coefficient reading, under which the strategy's\n    94\t\tline measurements are indexed by exactly the answers of the prescribed\n    95\t\tform, and its evaluation convention reproduces the universal\n    96\t\tquantification of the line-versus-point clauses of the win predicates\n    97\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate}).\n    98\t\\end{remark}\n    99\t\n   100\tThroughout this chapter $q = 2^k$ with $k$ odd is an admissible field size\n   101\t(Definition~\\ref{def:admissible-size}) and $n = 2^m$. Recall from\n   102\tChapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra} the low-degree code\n   103\t(see Definition~\\ref{def:low-degree-encoding}), which maps each vector\n   104\t$h \\in \\F_q^{n}$, with\n   105\tcoordinates indexed by binary strings of length $m$, to a polynomial\n   106\t$g_h \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ such that $g_h(u) = h_u$ for every\n   107\t$u \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$; for $x \\in \\F_q^m$ the associated vector\n   108\t$\\mathrm{ind}_m(x) \\in \\F_q^{n}$ satisfies\n   109\t$g_h(x) = h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(x)$ for all $h$. Recall also the finite\n   110\tfield trace $\\tr = \\tr_{q \\to 2} \\colon \\F_q \\to \\F_2$\n   111\t(see Definition~\\ref{def:subfield-trace}).\n   112\t\n   113\t\\begin{definition}[Commuting and anticommuting tuples]\n   114\t\t\\label{def:anticommuting-tuple}\n   115\t\t% The quantity gamma is introduced in the source at eq:gamma-value, inside\n   116\t\t% the completeness analysis of the Pauli basis test; that label has no\n   117\t\t% corresponding node here, so the definition below is self-contained.\n   118\t\t\\uses{def:low-degree-encoding, def:subfield-trace}\n   119\t\tFor a tuple\n   120\t\t$\\omega = (u_X, u_Z, r_X, r_Z) \\in (\\F_q^m)^2 \\times (\\F_q)^2$, let\n   121\t\t\\[\n   122\t\t\t\\gamma(\\omega) \\,=\\, \\tr\\big( (r_X \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u_X)) \\cdot (r_Z \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u_Z)) \\big)\\,,\n   123\t\t\\]\n   124\t\twhere $r_W \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u_W)$ is the scalar multiple of the\n   125\t\tvector $\\mathrm{ind}_m(u_W) \\in \\F_q^{n}$, the middle $\\cdot$ is the\n   245\t\\begin{definition}[Closeness of question-indexed operators]\n   246\t\t\\label{def:approx-question-indexed-operators}\n   247\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   248\t\tLet $\\{A^x\\}$ and $\\{B^x\\}$ be families of operators (not necessarily\n   249\t\tPOVM elements) indexed by questions $x \\in \\mathcal{X}$ but not by\n   250\t\tanswers, let $\\mu$ be a distribution over $\\mathcal{X}$, and let\n   251\t\t$\\ket{\\psi}$ be a state, both fixed by context. Write\n   252\t\t$A^x \\approx_\\delta B^x$ to denote\n   253\t\t\\[\n   254\t\t\t\\E_{x \\sim \\mu} \\bra{\\psi} (A^x - B^x)^\\dagger (A^x - B^x) \\ket{\\psi} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta)\\,.\n   255\t\t\\]\n   256\t\tThis extends the answer-indexed closeness of\n   257\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:povm-distance} to answer-free families of operators,\n   258\t\tsuch as observables; as there, the right-hand side allows a hidden\n   259\t\tuniversal constant.\n   260\t\\end{definition}\n   261\t\n   262\tThe following two lemmas relate the closeness between sets of operators and\n   263\tweighted sums of those operators.\n   264\t\n   265\t\\begin{lemma}\n   266\t\t\\label{lem:avg-closeness}\n   267\t\t\\uses{def:approx-question-indexed-operators}\n   268\t\tLet $\\{A^x\\}$ and $\\{B^x\\}$ be operators indexed by a finite set\n   269\t\t$\\mathcal{X}$, let $\\mu$ be a probability distribution over\n   270\t\t$\\mathcal{X}$, and let $\\{\\alpha_x\\}$ be complex numbers with\n   271\t\t$|\\alpha_x| \\leq 1$. If $A^x \\approx_\\delta B^x$ on average over\n   272\t\t$x \\sim \\mu$, then $A \\approx_\\delta B$ for the single operators\n   273\t\t$A = \\E_{x} \\alpha_x A^x$ and $B = \\E_{x} \\alpha_x B^x$, regarded as\n   274\t\tindexed by a one-element question set; quantitatively,\n   275\t\t% The closeness of Definition def:approx-question-indexed-operators\n   276\t\t% carries a hidden universal constant on both sides; the quantitative\n   277\t\t% sentence records the constant-free inequality the proof establishes.\n   278\t\t\\[\n   279\t\t\t\\big\\| (A - B) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\|^2\n   280\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, \\E_x \\big\\| (A^x - B^x) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\|^2\\,,\n   281\t\t\\]\n   282\t\tso the conclusion holds with the same implicit constant as the\n   283\t\thypothesis.\n   284\t\\end{lemma}\n   285\t\\begin{proof}\n   286\t\tExpand\n   287\t\t\\[\n   288\t\t\t\\Big\\| \\E_x \\alpha_x (A^x - B^x) \\ket{\\psi} \\Big\\|^2\n   289\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, \\Big( \\E_x \\big\\| (A^x - B^x) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\| \\Big)^2\n   290\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, \\E_x \\big\\| (A^x - B^x) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\|^2\\,,\n   291\t\t\\]\n   292\t\tusing the triangle inequality followed by Jensen's inequality; the\n   293\t\tright-hand side is at most $O(\\delta)$ by the hypothesis, with the same\n   294\t\timplicit constant.\n   295\t\\end{proof}\n   296\t\n   297\t\\begin{lemma}\n   298\t\t\\label{lem:povm-to-obs}\n   299\t\t\\uses{def:approx-question-indexed-operators, def:povm-distance}\n   300\t\tLet $\\mathcal{A}$ be a finite set, let $\\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}}$\n   301\t\tand $\\{B^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}}$ be POVMs, and let\n   302\t\t$\\{\\alpha_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}}$ be complex numbers on the unit\n   303\t\tcircle. Let $A^x = \\sum_a \\alpha_a A^x_a$ and\n   304\t\t$B^x = \\sum_a \\alpha_a B^x_a$. Then\n   305\t\t\\[\n   306\t\t\tA^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a \\quad\\text{implies}\\quad A^x \\approx_{\\delta'} B^x\n   307\t\t\\]\n   308\t\tfor $\\delta' = |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\delta$, where the hypothesis is the\n   309\t\tanswer-indexed closeness of Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance} and the\n   310\t\tconclusion is the question-indexed closeness of\n   311\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:approx-question-indexed-operators}.\n   312\t\\end{lemma}\n   313\t\\begin{proof}\n   314\t\t% The source ends this display with the exact bound |A| * delta; under the\n   315\t\t% O(.) reading of def:povm-distance the hypothesis only supplies O(delta),\n   316\t\t% and the constant is retained inside the O(.).\n   317\t\tExpand\n   318\t\t\\[\n   319\t\t\t\\E_x \\Big\\| \\sum_a \\alpha_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi} \\Big\\|^2\n   320\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, \\E_x \\Big( \\sum_a \\big\\| (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\| \\Big)^2\n   321\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\E_x \\sum_a \\big\\| (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\|^2\n   322\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, O\\big( |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\delta \\big)\\,,\n   323\t\t\\]\n   324\t\tusing the triangle inequality followed by the Cauchy--Schwarz\n   325\t\tinequality; the last step is the hypothesis\n   326\t\t$A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$, whose bound carries a hidden universal\n   327\t\tconstant (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}), and the conclusion\n   328\t\tholds with the same implicit constant.\n   329\t\\end{proof}\n   330\t\n   331\tThe following lemma shows that POVMs that are approximately projective, in\n   332\tthe sense of being self-consistent, are close to exact projective\n   333\tmeasurements.\n   334\t\n   335\t\\begin{lemma}[Orthonormalization lemma]\n   336\t\t\\label{lem:ortho}\n   337\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   338\t\tLet $0 \\leq \\delta \\leq 1$. There exists a function\n   339\t\t$\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta) = O\\big(\\delta^{1/4}\\big)$ such that the\n   340\t\tfollowing holds. Let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a state on\n   341\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ where $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$\n   342\t\tand $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ are finite dimensional, and let $\\{Q_a\\}$ and\n   343\t\t$\\{R_a\\}$ be POVMs on $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$\n   344\t\trespectively such that\n   345\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-cond}\n   346\t\t\tQ_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\simeq_\\delta\\, \\Id_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot R_a\\,.\n   347\t\t\\end{equation}\n   348\t\tThen there exists a projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$ on\n   349\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ such that, with respect to $\\ket{\\psi}$,\n   350\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-ccl}\n   351\t\t\tP_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\approx_{\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta)}\\, Q_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}}\\,.\n   352\t\t\\end{equation}\n   353\t\\end{lemma}\n   354\t\\begin{proof}\n   355\t\tThis lemma is imported: it first appears in~\\cite{KV11}, and a\n   356\t\tself-contained proof is given in the reference [ML20] cited\n   357\t\tin~\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. The proof is not reproduced here.\n   358\t\\end{proof}\n   359\t\n   360\t\\section{Strategies}\n   361\t\\label{sec:commutation}\n   362\t\n   363\t\\begin{definition}[The strategy under analysis]\n   364\t\t\\label{def:projective-strategy-setup}\n   365\t\t\\uses{def:admissible, def:pauli-question-distribution, def:pauli-win-predicate, def:tensor-product-strategy, def:tensor-product-value, def:projective-strategy-general, thm:naimark}\n   810\t\t(see Lemma~\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation}). The two signs cancel, so\n   811\t\t$\\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X)\\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z) \\approx_{\\sqrt{\\eps}} \\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z)\\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X)$\n   812\t\ton average over uniformly random $\\omega$, giving the approximate\n   813\t\tcommutation with $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Anticom}} = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   814\t\\end{proof}\n   815\t\n   816\t\\begin{definition}[Expanded line measurements]\n   817\t\t\\label{def:expanded-line-measurement}\n   818\t\t% In the source this measurement is defined inside the proof of the lemma\n   819\t\t% labelled lem:qld-comm-line-cons (lines 552-556); it is recorded as a\n   820\t\t% standalone definition because Chapter chap:qpbt-combining uses the\n   821\t\t% concrete measurement, not merely its existence.\n   822\t\t\\uses{def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:expanded-observables, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:low-degree-encoding, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, def:line, def:pauli-win-predicate}\n   823\t\tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, a line $\\ell$, and $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$,\n   824\t\tdefine\n   825\t\t\\[\n   826\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\n   827\t\t\t\\,=\\, \\sum_{\\substack{f',f'' \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell) \\\\ f' + f'' = f}}\n   828\t\t\tM^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{f'} \\ot \\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}\\,,\n   829\t\t\\]\n   830\t\twhere the projector $\\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}$, acting on register\n   831\t\t$\\mathsf{A}'$ (or on $\\mathsf{A}''$, following the register conventions\n   832\t\tof Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-observables}), is the outcome-$f''$\n   833\t\tprojector of the following projective measurement: measure all $n$\n   834\t\tqudits in the basis $W$, obtaining an outcome $h \\in \\F_q^{n}$, and\n   835\t\treturn the polynomial $f''$ obtained by substituting the\n   836\t\tparameterization of $\\ell$ into the low-degree encoding\n   837\t\t$g_h \\colon \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$\n   838\t\t(see Definition~\\ref{def:low-degree-encoding}): writing $(u_0,v)$ for\n   839\t\tthe base point and direction of $\\ell$, the outcome $f''$ is\n   840\t\t$g_h(u_0 + tv)$, expanded as a polynomial in the variable $t$ from the\n   841\t\tcoefficient representation of $g_h$. Since $g_h$ has individual degree\n   842\t\tat most $d$, hence total degree at most $md$, the substituted\n   843\t\tpolynomial has degree at most $md$ in $t$, so\n   844\t\t$f'' \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$. The strategy measurement\n   845\t\t$\\{M^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{f'}\\}$ is indexed by the answers of the\n   846\t\tprescribed form (Definition~\\ref{def:pauli-win-predicate}): for a\n   847\t\tdiagonal line these are the elements of $\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$, and for an\n   848\t\taxis-parallel line the elements of $\\deg_d(\\ell)$, regarded as a\n   849\t\tmeasurement with outcomes in $\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$ through the inclusion\n   850\t\t$\\deg_d(\\ell) \\subseteq \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$ of\n   851\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:ideg-deg-polynomials}, with the zero operator\n   852\t\tassigned to outcomes outside $\\deg_d(\\ell)$. For an axis-parallel\n   853\t\t$\\ell$, with direction $e_i$, the substituted polynomial also has\n   854\t\tdegree at most $d$ in $t$ --- its degree is the individual degree of\n   855\t\t$g_h$ in the $i$-th variable --- so\n   856\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f = 0$ unless $f \\in \\deg_d(\\ell)$.\n   857\t\tIn all cases\n   858\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)}$ is a\n   859\t\tprojective measurement.\n   860\t\\end{definition}\n   861\t\n   862\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency of the expanded line measurements]\n   863\t\t\\label{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}\n   864\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:line-point-dist, def:approx-question-indexed-operators, def:povm-distance, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   865\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\poly(\\eps)$\n   866\t\tsuch that the following holds, with respect to the state\n   867\t\t$\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state}. For all\n   868\t\t$W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and lines $\\ell$ there exists a projective measurement\n   869\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)}$,\n   870\t\tacting on registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$ or\n   871\t\t$\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, such that:\n   872\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   873\t\t\t\\item \\label{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\n   874\t\t\t(\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and\n   875\t\t\t$r \\in \\F_q$ (the relation does not depend on $r$), on average over\n   876\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   877\t\t\tdistribution (Definition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist}),\n   878\t\t\t\\[\n   879\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   880\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   881\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   882\t\t\t\\]\n   883\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   884\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}\n   885\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements I}) On average over\n   886\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   887\t\t\tdistribution,\n   888\t\t\t\\[\n   889\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   890\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   891\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   892\t\t\t\t\\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f(u)})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   893\t\t\t\\]\n   894\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   895\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2}\n   896\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements II}) On average over\n   897\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   898\t\t\tdistribution,\n   899\t\t\t\\[\n   900\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   901\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   902\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   903\t\t\t\\]\n   904\t\t\twhere\n   905\t\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]} = \\sum_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell) \\colon f(u)=a} \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f$\n   906\t\t\tand the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$.\n   907\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   908\t\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also\n   909\t\thold. The proof exhibits the measurements of\n   910\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement} as witnesses, with\n   911\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} holding with error $\\eps$ and\n   912\t\titems~\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\n   913\t\tand~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2} with\n   914\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   915\t\\end{lemma}\n   916\t\\begin{proof}\n   917\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-point-measurement, lem:qld-win-implications, fact:data-processing, fact:add-a-proj2, fact:agreement, def:line-point-dist, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:pauli-win-predicate}\n   918\t\tTake $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}$ to be the measurement of\n   919\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement}.\n   920\t\tItem~\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons} follows from the fact that the\n   921\t\toriginal line measurements $\\{M^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}$ are\n   922\t\t$\\eps$-self-consistent between registers $\\mathsf{A}$ and $\\mathsf{B}$\n   923\t\tin $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ (item~\\ref{enu:qld-cons} of\n   924\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}), the Pauli projections\n   925\t\t$\\{\\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}\\}$ are perfectly self-consistent between\n   926\t\tregisters $\\mathsf{A}'$ and $\\mathsf{A}''$, and the data processing\n   927\t\tinequality (Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}). For\n   928\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}: for the outcomes $f'$ that\n   929\t\tevaluate at $u$, insert the projection\n   930\t\t$M^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=f'(u)]}$ by\n   931\t\tprojectivity, replace it by\n   932\t\t$(M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f'(u)})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$ using the\n   933\t\tline-point consistency (item~\\ref{enu:qld-ld} of\n   934\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}) together with\n   935\t\tLemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj2}, adjoin the exactly consistent projector\n   936\t\t$\\tau^{W,u}_{f''(u)}$ on register $\\mathsf{A}''$, and recombine by the\n   937\t\tconvolution form of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}.\n   938\t\tOutcomes without an evaluation at $u$ occur only when $\\ell$ has zero\n   939\t\tdirection, where $\\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}$ vanishes unless $f''$ is a\n   940\t\tconstant polynomial, so that $f = f' + f''$ evaluates at $u$ exactly\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/0001/cf4cfa6eb26ad593c4e130d033b54e6a455a1ae1/diff.patch:5166:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_87","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '230,325p'\"","aggregated_output":"   230\t\ttogether with the analogous identity for $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$.\n   231\t\\end{lemma}\n   232\t\\begin{proof}\n   233\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, lem:tildew-product-form, lem:twisted-commutation, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:indicator-vector, def:decoding-map, def:generalized-pauli}\n   234\t\tSince the measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ is projective (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}) and the $\\tau^X, \\tau^Z$ observables are self-inverse,\n   235\t\t\\[\n   236\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger = \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}''} = \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}\\;,\n   237\t\t\\]\n   238\t\tand the same computation gives $V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger V_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\Id$, so $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ is unitary; likewise $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$. For \\eqref{eq:v-swap-obs-conjugation}, conjugate the product form \\eqref{eq:tildewj-product-form} of $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:tildew-product-form}): by the orthogonality of the projectors $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$, only the diagonal terms survive, and the twisted commutation relation (Lemma~\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation})\n   239\t\t\\[\n   240\t\t\t\\tau^X(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)) \\, \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X)) \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = (-1)^{\\tr( e_j(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\, \\tau^X(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)) \\, \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X))\n   241\t\t\\]\n   242\t\tcancels the sign $(-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))}$ term by term, leaving $\\sum_{g_X,g_Z} \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z} \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\Id \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$. For \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}, expand $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$ by \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu}; by the orthogonality of the $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$, conjugation replaces, in the term of $\\hat{S}^W_g$, each projector $\\tau^W_h$ by $\\tau^W_{h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)}$, using the identities $\\tau^X(s)\\, \\tau^Z_h \\,\\tau^X(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^Z_{h+s}$ and $\\tau^Z(s)\\, \\tau^X_h\\, \\tau^Z(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^X_{h+s}$. Substituting $h' = h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)$ turns the constraint $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = (\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)) - a$ into $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = -a = a$, since $\\F_q$ has characteristic $2$; the quantity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly, so no relation between it and $g(u)$ is used (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}). Since $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_{h'}(u)$ \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}, summing over $g$ gives \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}; the computation for $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ is identical.\n   243\t\\end{proof}\n   244\t\n   245\t\\begin{lemma}[Extraction of the EPR state and the Pauli observables]\\label{lem:qld-unitary}\n   246\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, def:v-swap-unitary, def:expanded-state, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:generalized-pauli, def:EPR, def:povm-distance}\n   247\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$, where $\\delta_S$ is the function of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, and there exist unitaries $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ acting on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ and $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$ acting on registers $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, such that the following hold.\n   248\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   249\t\t\t\\item There exists a state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ such that\n   250\t\t\t\\[\n   251\t\t\t\t\\bigl\\| V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\bigr\\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\\;.\n   252\t\t\t\\]\n   253\t\t\t\\item For $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n   254\t\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   255\t\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''}\\;, \\\\\n   256\t\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   257\t\t\t\\end{align*}\n   258\t\t\twhere the $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''}$, with the answer summation over $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$ and no average over questions.\n   259\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   260\t\tThe unitaries may be taken to be $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:v-swap-unitary}.\n   261\t\\end{lemma}\n   262\t\\begin{proof}\n   263\t\t\\uses{def:v-swap-unitary, lem:v-swap-conjugation, lem:tildew-product-form, lem:qld-construct-the-paulis, lem:qld-win-implications, fact:triangle-for-simeq, def:bracket, lem:cancellation, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree, lem:qld-4-7}\n   264\t\tFor the first item, the self-consistency of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j$ (Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}) is equivalent, since these observables are Hermitian involutions (Lemma~\\ref{lem:tildew-product-form}), to $\\E_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}} \\E_{\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M} \\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ot \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2$; setting $\\ket{\\vartheta} = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ and using the exact conjugation identities of Lemma~\\ref{lem:v-swap-conjugation}, this becomes $\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2$ for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, where $H_W = \\E_j \\E_{\\tilde{u}} \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\bigl( \\E_{s \\in \\F_q} \\tau^W(s) \\ot \\tau^W(s) \\bigr)^{\\ot M}$ by the tensor factorization of $\\tau^W(\\tilde{u})$ and the invariance of the uniform average over $s$ under $s \\mapsto e_j s$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:cancellation}, $\\E_{s,s'} \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') \\ot \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') = \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ on each pair of qudits, whence $H_X H_Z = \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$. The bounds $\\| (\\Id - H_W)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq \\delta_S$ and $\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W^2 \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - 2\\delta_S$, combined through the triangle inequality $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq 2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ (see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}), give $\\bra{\\vartheta} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - 4\\delta_S$.\n   265\t\t% Case distinction added: the source normalizes the projected vector\n   266\t\t% without ensuring that it is nonzero; see Remark rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip.\n   267\t\tIf $\\delta_S > 1/8$, then, after enlarging the universal constant in $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$, both items of the lemma hold with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ an arbitrary unit state, since their left-hand sides never exceed $4$; assume therefore $\\delta_S \\leq 1/8$. Then the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ has squared norm at least $1 - 4\\delta_S \\geq 1/2 > 0$, and $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ may be defined as its normalization; the overlap of $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ equals the norm of that vector and is at least $\\sqrt{1 - 4\\delta_S} \\geq 1 - 4\\delta_S$, which yields the first item with a bound of $8\\delta_S = O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$. For the second item, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} gives, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$, $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\eps \\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ and $\\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\simeq_\\eps M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id$, where $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} = \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = a} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h$; combining these with Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} by Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle-for-simeq}, using $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$, yields $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}} \\Id \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$. Writing $\\ket{\\Delta} = \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$, the quantity to be bounded for the $\\mathrm{A}$-side relation equals $2 - 2\\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\Delta}$, since $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} = (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$. Replacing $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ by the averaged coarse-graining $\\E_u \\sum_{h' : g_{h'}(u) = g_h(u)} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ costs at most $md/q$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree} (distinct $h \\neq h'$ have distinct encodings $g_h \\neq g_{h'}$, of total degree at most $md$), and by \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the resulting sum equals $\\E_u \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger \\ket{\\Delta}$. Exchanging $\\ket{\\Delta}$ for $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ costs $O(\\delta_S^{1/4})$ by the first item, and the resulting expression $\\E_u \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ is at least $1 - O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$ by the $\\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}}$ relation above. Altogether the distance is $O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$; the $\\mathrm{B}$-side relation is proved identically.\n   268\t\\end{proof}\n   269\t\n   270\t\\begin{remark}[Two steps in the source proof of the extraction lemma]\\label{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}\n   271\t\tIn the source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, the triangle inequality gives $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq \\| (\\Id - H_X)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| + \\| (\\Id - H_Z)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq 2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$, so that $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq 4\\delta_S$; the source instead asserts the bound $2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ for the \\emph{squared} norm, which holds only when $\\sqrt{\\delta_S} \\leq 1/2$, and consequently carries the term $\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ into an overlap bound of $1 - 2\\delta_S - \\sqrt{\\delta_S}$. The proof above uses the corrected bound $4\\delta_S$, which gives the overlap bound $1 - 4\\delta_S$ and leaves the final bound $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}} = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ unchanged. The source proof also normalizes the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ without ensuring that it is nonzero; the case distinction on $\\delta_S$ in the proof above supplies this, the conclusions of the lemma being trivial when $\\delta_S$ exceeds a universal constant.\n   272\t\\end{remark}\n   273\t\n   274\t\\section{Proof of the soundness of the Pauli basis test}\n   275\t\n   276\tWe can now prove the soundness theorem for the Pauli basis test, stated as Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-test}.\n   277\t\n   278\t\\begin{proof}\n   279\t\t\\proves{thm:pauli}\n   280\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-unitary, def:v-swap-unitary, thm:naimark, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:EPR, def:povm-distance}\n   281\t\tLet $(\\ket{\\psi},M)$ be a strategy for $\\mathfrak{G}^{\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ that succeeds with probability at least $1 - \\eps$, where $\\ket{\\psi}$ is a bipartite state on registers $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$. By Naimark's theorem (Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}, as formulated in~\\cite[Theorem 5.1]{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}), at the cost of extending $\\ket{\\psi}$ by sufficiently many ancilla qubits initialized in the state $\\ket{0}$, we may assume that the strategy is projective and that the state is padded as in the conventions of Definition~\\ref{def:projective-strategy-setup}. Let $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ be the unitary acting on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ given by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, and let $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}$ be the isometry mapping register $\\mathrm{A}$ to registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$, where $\\mathrm{A}'$ and $\\mathrm{A}''$ are isomorphic to $(\\C^q)^{\\ot M}$, defined by\n   282\t\t\\[\n   283\t\t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}\\colon \\ket{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\mapsto V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\bigl(\\ket{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot (\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}' \\mathrm{A}''}\\bigr)\\;;\n   284\t\t\\]\n   285\t\tdefine $\\phi_{\\mathrm{B}}$ analogously. Since the expanded state $\\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state} is exactly $\\ket{\\psi}$ with the halves of $M$ pairs $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ appended locally in registers $\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ and $\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, we have $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi} = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$. Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary} therefore yields a state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ such that\n   286\t\t\\[\n   287\t\t\t\\bigl\\| \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} \\bigr\\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\\;.\n   288\t\t\\]\n   289\t\t% Transfer step added: the source (lines 1864--1875 of\n   290\t\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex) passes directly from the\n   291\t\t% conjugation estimates of lem:qld-unitary, which concern the\n   292\t\t% unitarily conjugated operators V (M_h ot Id) V^dagger, to the\n   293\t\t% corresponding estimates for the isometry phi, whose conjugation\n   294\t\t% inserts the projection onto its range; the range-projection\n   295\t\t% argument below supplies the missing step.\n   296\t\tItem~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary} concerns the unitarily conjugated operators $N^W_h = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger$, whereas conjugation by the isometry $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}$ additionally inserts the projection $Q_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\bigl(\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger$ onto the range of $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}$: since $(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $\\bigl(\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}$ act on disjoint registers and hence commute,\n   297\t\t\\[\n   298\t\t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger\n   299\t\t\t= V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\Bigl( (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\bigl(\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\Bigr) \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger\n   300\t\t\t= N^W_h \\, Q_{\\mathrm{A}} = Q_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, N^W_h\\;.\n   301\t\t\\]\n   302\t\tThe estimates transfer from $N^W_h$ to $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger$ as follows. Write $\\ket{\\Delta} = \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ and $\\ket{\\vartheta} = \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi}$. Since $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\Id$, the projection $\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}$ annihilates $\\ket{\\vartheta}$, so the displayed bound above gives\n   303\t\t\\[\n   304\t\t\t\\bigl\\| (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) \\ket{\\Delta} \\bigr\\|^2\n   305\t\t\t= \\bigl\\| (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) (\\ket{\\Delta} - \\ket{\\vartheta}) \\bigr\\|^2\n   306\t\t\t\\leq \\bigl\\| \\ket{\\Delta} - \\ket{\\vartheta} \\bigr\\|^2\n   307\t\t\t\\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\\;.\n   308\t\t\\]\n   309\t\tSince the strategy is projective, the operators $N^W_h$, for $h \\in \\F_q^M$, are mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity, so $\\sum_h \\| N^W_h \\ket{\\xi} \\|^2 = \\| \\ket{\\xi} \\|^2$ for every vector $\\ket{\\xi}$. Decomposing\n   310\t\t\\[\n   311\t\t\t\\bigl( \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger - (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\bigr) \\ket{\\Delta}\n   312\t\t\t= \\bigl( N^W_h - (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\bigr) \\ket{\\Delta} - N^W_h (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) \\ket{\\Delta}\n   313\t\t\\]\n   314\t\tand summing the squared norms over $h$ via $\\| \\xi - \\xi' \\|^2 \\leq 2\\|\\xi\\|^2 + 2\\|\\xi'\\|^2$, the first terms contribute $O(\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}})$ by Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary} and the second terms contribute at most $2 \\| (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) \\ket{\\Delta} \\|^2 \\leq 2\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$; hence, for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n   315\t\t\\[\n   316\t\t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''}\n   317\t\t\t\\qquad \\text{and, by the identical argument,} \\qquad\n   318\t\t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger \\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   319\t\t\\]\n   320\t\twhere the $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ and the answer summation is over $h \\in \\F_q^M$; the constant implicit in $\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}}$ (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}) absorbs the factors accumulated above. This shows that the game $\\mathfrak{G}^{\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ is a self-test, with robustness $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q)$, for the canonical Pauli strategy of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pauli-completeness}. Identifying the registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ with $\\mathrm{A}'$ and $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ with $\\mathrm{B}'$ matches the statement of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}, in which $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}\\colon \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\to \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}''}$. Finally, unpacking the dependence on the parameters $\\eps, m, d, q$, the function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ has the form $a(md)^a(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for universal constants $a > 1$ and $0 < b < 1$; see Remark~\\ref{rem:pauli-robustness-form} for the reconciliation of this form with the statement of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}.\n   321\t\\end{proof}\n   322\t\n   323\t\\begin{remark}[Norm conventions in the robustness bound]\\label{rem:pauli-robustness-form}\n   324\t\tThe proof above, following the source, establishes the first conclusion of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} in the squared form $\\| \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} \\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$, whereas the theorem bounds the unsquared norm by $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q)$. The two are reconciled by replacing the exponent $b$ with $b/2$ and enlarging $a$, since the class of functions $a(md)^a(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ is closed under square roots up to such adjustments of the universal constants. Similarly, the closing sentence of the source proof asserts constants $a > 1$ and $0 < b < 1$, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} requires only $a \\geq 1$ and $0 < b < 1$; the former implies the latter.\n   325\t\\end{remark}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
