ts measurements (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}) at cost $\\eps$; the exact identity $\\ket{\\hat\\psi} = \\sum_{h'} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ of the EPR registers; Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} twice more (once through a Cauchy--Schwarz detour of cost $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ using the self-consistency of the $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}); and finally a Schwartz--Zippel step (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}, for polynomials of total degree at most $md$) which replaces the event $(g'-g_{h'})(u) = (g-g_h)(u)$ by the polynomial identity $g'-g_{h'} = g-g_h$ at cost $md/q$. The resulting expression\n \t\\[\n \t\t\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h \\\\ (\\mathrm{Dec}(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}}\n \t\t\\Bigl((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\tau_h^W)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\Bigr) \\cdot \\Bigl( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\Bigr)\n \t\\]\n \tis symmetric under exchanging $(g,h)$ with $(g',h')$: since $\\mathrm{Dec}$ is linear with $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_{h}) = h$ for all $h \\in \\F_q^M$ (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}), the constraint $g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h$ implies $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) - h = \\mathrm{Dec}(g') - h'$, so the two constraint sets coincide. The same derivation therefore bounds the distance of the displayed expression to $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$, and \\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons} follows after absorbing the error terms $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ and $O(md/q)$ into $\\delta_S$. Finally, since $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}$ is projective, Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, data processing along the map $a \\mapsto \\tr(e_j a)$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}), and Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} yield $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$ with answer summation over $b \\in \\F_2$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:povm-to-obs} converts this into the claimed self-consistency of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$.\n \\end{proof}\n \n-The following lemma was used in several derivations above. It is stated after Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} to follow the source, but its proof depends only on Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}, so there is no circularity.\n+% Following the source, the next lemma is stated after\n+% lem:qld-construct-the-paulis, whose proof invokes it; its own proof\n+% depends only on lem:qld-4-7 and lem:qld-comm-cons, so there is no\n+% circularity.\n+Lemmas~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} and~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons} imply the following two estimates, which were used in the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}.\n \n \\begin{lemma}[The marginals absorb the point measurements]\\label{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper}\n \t\\uses{def:s-w-marginals, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:povm-distance, lem:qld-4-7, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n \tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n \t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-sg-cons}\n \t\t\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id\n \t\\end{equation}\n \tand\n \t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-sg-cons2}\n \t\t\\bigl(\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{g(u)})\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} 0\\;,\n@@ -266,31 +270,56 @@ The following lemma was used in several derivations above. It is stated after Le\n \\begin{remark}[Two steps in the source proof of the extraction lemma]\\label{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}\n \tIn the source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, the triangle inequality gives $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq \\| (\\Id - H_X)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| + \\| (\\Id - H_Z)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq 2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$, so that $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq 4\\delta_S$; the source instead asserts the bound $2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ for the \\emph{squared} norm, which holds only when $\\sqrt{\\delta_S} \\leq 1/2$, and consequently carries the term $\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ into an overlap bound of $1 - 2\\delta_S - \\sqrt{\\delta_S}$. The proof above uses the corrected bound $4\\delta_S$, which gives the overlap bound $1 - 4\\delta_S$ and leaves the final bound $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}} = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ unchanged. The source proof also normalizes the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ without ensuring that it is nonzero; the case distinction on $\\delta_S$ in the proof above supplies this, the conclusions of the lemma being trivial when $\\delta_S$ exceeds a universal constant.\n \\end{remark}\n \n \\section{Proof of the soundness of the Pauli basis test}\n \n We can now prove the soundness theorem for the Pauli basis test, stated as Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-test}.\n \n \\begin{proof}\n \t\\proves{thm:pauli}\n-\t\\uses{lem:qld-unitary, def:v-swap-unitary, thm:naimark, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:EPR}\n+\t\\uses{lem:qld-unitary, def:v-swap-unitary, thm:naimark, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:EPR, def:povm-distance}\n \tLet $(\\ket{\\psi},M)$ be a strategy for $\\mathfrak{G}^{\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ that succeeds with probability at least $1 - \\eps$, where $\\ket{\\psi}$ is a bipartite state on registers $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$. By Naimark's theorem (Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}, as formulated in~\\cite[Theorem 5.1]{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}), at the cost of extending $\\ket{\\psi}$ by sufficiently many ancilla qubits initialized in the state $\\ket{0}$, we may assume that the strategy is projective and that the state is padded as in the conventions of Definition~\\ref{def:projective-strategy-setup}. Let $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ be the unitary acting on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ given by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, and let $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}$ be the isometry mapping register $\\mathrm{A}$ to registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$, where $\\mathrm{A}'$ and $\\mathrm{A}''$ are isomorphic to $(\\C^q)^{\\ot M}$, defined by\n \t\\[\n \t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}\\colon \\ket{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\mapsto V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\bigl(\\ket{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot (\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}' \\mathrm{A}''}\\bigr)\\;;\n \t\\]\n \tdefine $\\phi_{\\mathrm{B}}$ analogously. Since the expanded state $\\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state} is exactly $\\ket{\\psi}$ with the halves of $M$ pairs $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ appended locally in registers $\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ and $\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, we have $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi} = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$. Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary} therefore yields a state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ such that\n \t\\[\n-\t\t\\bigl\\| \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} \\bigr\\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\n+\t\t\\bigl\\| \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} \\bigr\\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\\;.\n \t\\]\n-\tand, for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n+\t% Transfer step added: the source (lines 1864--1875 of\n+\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex) passes directly from the\n+\t% conjugation estimates of lem:qld-unitary, which concern the\n+\t% unitarily conjugated operators V (M_h ot Id) V^dagger, to the\n+\t% corresponding estimates for the isometry phi, whose conjugation\n+\t% inserts the projection onto its range; the range-projection\n+\t% argument below supplies the missing step.\n+\tItem~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary} concerns the unitarily conjugated operators $N^W_h = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger$, whereas conjugation by the isometry $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}$ additionally inserts the projection $Q_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\bigl(\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger$ onto the range of $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}$: since $(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $\\bigl(\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}$ act on disjoint registers and hence commute,\n+\t\\[\n+\t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger\n+\t\t= V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\Bigl( (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\bigl(\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\Bigr) \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger\n+\t\t= N^W_h \\, Q_{\\mathrm{A}} = Q_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, N^W_h\\;.\n+\t\\]\n+\tThe estimates transfer from $N^W_h$ to $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger$ as follows. Write $\\ket{\\Delta} = \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ and $\\ket{\\vartheta} = \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi}$. Since $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\Id$, the projection $\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}$ annihilates $\\ket{\\vartheta}$, so the displayed bound above gives\n+\t\\[\n+\t\t\\bigl\\| (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) \\ket{\\Delta} \\bigr\\|^2\n+\t\t= \\bigl\\| (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) (\\ket{\\Delta} - \\ket{\\vartheta}) \\bigr\\|^2\n+\t\t\\leq \\bigl\\| \\ket{\\Delta} - \\ket{\\vartheta} \\bigr\\|^2\n+\t\t\\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\\;.\n+\t\\]\n+\tSince the strategy is projective, the operators $N^W_h$, for $h \\in \\F_q^M$, are mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity, so $\\sum_h \\| N^W_h \\ket{\\xi} \\|^2 = \\| \\ket{\\xi} \\|^2$ for every vector $\\ket{\\xi}$. Decomposing\n+\t\\[\n+\t\t\\bigl( \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger - (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\bigr) \\ket{\\Delta}\n+\t\t= \\bigl( N^W_h - (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\bigr) \\ket{\\Delta} - N^W_h (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) \\ket{\\Delta}\n+\t\\]\n+\tand summing the squared norms over $h$ via $\\| \\xi - \\xi' \\|^2 \\leq 2\\|\\xi\\|^2 + 2\\|\\xi'\\|^2$, the first terms contribute $O(\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}})$ by Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary} and the second terms contribute at most $2 \\| (\\Id - Q_{\\mathrm{A}}) \\ket{\\Delta} \\|^2 \\leq 2\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$; hence, for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n \t\\[\n \t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''}\n-\t\t\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n+\t\t\\qquad \\text{and, by the identical argument,} \\qquad\n \t\t\\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h)_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger \\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n \t\\]\n-\twhere the $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ and the answer summation is over $h \\in \\F_q^M$. This shows that the game $\\mathfrak{G}^{\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ is a self-test, with robustness $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q)$, for the canonical Pauli strategy of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pauli-completeness}. Identifying the registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ with $\\mathrm{A}'$ and $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ with $\\mathrm{B}'$ matches the statement of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}, in which $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}\\colon \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\to \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}''}$. Finally, unpacking the dependence on the parameters $\\eps, m, d, q$, the function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ has the form $a(md)^a(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for universal constants $a > 1$ and $0 < b < 1$; see Remark~\\ref{rem:pauli-robustness-form} for the reconciliation of this form with the statement of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}.\n+\twhere the $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ and the answer summation is over $h \\in \\F_q^M$; the constant implicit in $\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}}$ (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}) absorbs the factors accumulated above. This shows that the game $\\mathfrak{G}^{\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ is a self-test, with robustness $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q)$, for the canonical Pauli strategy of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pauli-completeness}. Identifying the registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ with $\\mathrm{A}'$ and $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ with $\\mathrm{B}'$ matches the statement of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}, in which $\\phi_{\\mathrm{A}}\\colon \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\to \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\mathcal{H}_{\\mathrm{A}''}$. Finally, unpacking the dependence on the parameters $\\eps, m, d, q$, the function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ has the form $a(md)^a(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for universal constants $a > 1$ and $0 < b < 1$; see Remark~\\ref{rem:pauli-robustness-form} for the reconciliation of this form with the statement of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}.\n \\end{proof}\n \n \\begin{remark}[Norm conventions in the robustness bound]\\label{rem:pauli-robustness-form}\n \tThe proof above, following the source, establishes the first conclusion of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} in the squared form $\\| \\phi_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\psi} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} \\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$, whereas the theorem bounds the unsquared norm by $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q)$. The two are reconciled by replacing the exponent $b$ with $b/2$ and enlarging $a$, since the class of functions $a(md)^a(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ is closed under square roots up to such adjustments of the universal constants. Similarly, the closing sentence of the source proof asserts constants $a > 1$ and $0 < b < 1$, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} requires only $a \\geq 1$ and $0 < b < 1$; the former implies the latter.\n \\end{remark}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/0001/cf4cfa6eb26ad593c4e130d033b54e6a455a1ae1/diff.patch:5190:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_98","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex | sed -n '315,455p;720,1012p'\"","aggregated_output":"   315\t\terror, a $\\poly(\\eps, md/q)$ function, and the identical argument with\n   316\t\tthe registers modified gives all symmetric equivalents.\n   317\t\\end{proof}\n   318\t\n   319\t\\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}\n   320\t\\label{sec:apply-ldt}\n   321\t\n   322\tThe combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next\n   323\tmerged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that\n   324\tencode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is\n   325\tdone by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking\n   326\tthe quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test\n   327\t(Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The instantiation at dimension $2m+2$\n   328\trequires that $2m+2$ divide $q$; the statements below carry this hypothesis\n   329\texplicitly, and Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} discusses its\n   330\trelationship to the admissibility of $(q,m,d)$.\n   331\t\n   332\t\\begin{definition}[The combining map]\n   333\t\t\\label{def:combine-map}\n   334\t\t\\uses{def:polyfunc, def:line}\n   335\t\tFor $f, g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$, the polynomial\n   336\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,2m+2}(\\F_q)$ is defined by\n   337\t\t\\[\n   338\t\t\t\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g}(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta)\n   339\t\t\t= \\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(z)\\,,\n   340\t\t\t\\qquad x \\in \\F_q^m,\\ z \\in \\F_q^m,\\ \\alpha \\in \\F_q,\\\n   341\t\t\t\\beta \\in \\F_q\\,.\n   342\t\t\\]\n   343\t\tThe combining map is also defined for polynomials restricted to lines.\n   344\t\tLet $\\ell$ be a line in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ with intercept\n   345\t\t$u = (u_X, u_Z, u_\\alpha, u_\\beta)$ and slope\n   346\t\t$v = (v_X, v_Z, v_\\alpha, v_\\beta)$, and let $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$ be\n   347\t\tlines in $\\F_q^m$ such that for every point\n   348\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\ell$ it holds that $x \\in \\ell_X$ and\n   349\t\t$z \\in \\ell_Z$. Then for any two polynomials\n   350\t\t$f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X)$ and $g \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$, the polynomial\n   351\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$ is defined by\n   352\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   353\t\t\t\\label{eq:combine-lines}\n   354\t\t\t\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g}(u + t \\cdot v)\n   355\t\t\t= (u_\\alpha + t \\cdot v_\\alpha)\\, f(u_X + t \\cdot v_X)\n   356\t\t\t+ (u_\\beta + t \\cdot v_\\beta)\\, g(u_Z + t \\cdot v_Z)\\,,\n   357\t\t\t\\qquad t \\in \\F_q\\,.\n   358\t\t\\end{equation}\n   359\t\tThe degree bound holds because $f$ and $g$, restricted to lines, are\n   360\t\tpolynomials of degree at most $md$ in the line parameter, and each is\n   361\t\tmultiplied by an affine factor of degree at most one.\n   362\t\\end{definition}\n   363\t\n   364\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined point measurement on the extended coordinates]\n   365\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-12}\n   366\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, def:expanded-state, def:expanded-point-measurement,\n   367\t\t\tdef:povm-distance, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   368\t\tFor all $x, z \\in \\F_q^m$, $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\F_q$, and\n   369\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ there exists a\n   370\t\tprojective measurement\n   371\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c}\\}_{c \\in \\F_q}$ acting on\n   372\t\t$\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$ such that the following holds, where\n   373\t\t$\\delta_Q$ is the function of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}:\n   374\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   375\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) On average over uniformly random\n   376\t\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n   377\t\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   378\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-self-cons}\n   379\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   380\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   381\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,.\n   382\t\t\t\\end{equation}\n   383\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with $\\hat{M}$}) On average over uniformly\n   384\t\t\trandom $(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n   385\t\t\t\\begin{gather}\n   386\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat}\n   387\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   388\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   389\t\t\t\t\\Big( \\sum_{\\substack{a,b:\\\\ \\alpha a + \\beta b = c}}\n   390\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a} \\,\n   391\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b}\n   392\t\t\t\t\\Big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,, \\\\\n   393\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat2}\n   394\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   395\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   396\t\t\t\t\\Big( \\sum_{\\substack{a,b:\\\\ \\alpha a + \\beta b = c}}\n   397\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\,\n   398\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   399\t\t\t\t\\Big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,.\n   400\t\t\t\\end{gather}\n   401\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   402\t\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also hold.\n   403\t\\end{lemma}\n   404\t\\begin{proof}\n   405\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, fact:data-processing}\n   406\t\tDefine\n   407\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c}\n   408\t\t= \\sum_{a,b \\,:\\, \\alpha a + \\beta b = c} \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$,\n   409\t\ta coarse-graining of the projective measurement of\n   410\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}, hence itself projective. A uniformly random\n   411\t\tpoint of $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ has uniformly random coordinates $(x,z)$, so the\n   412\t\tself-consistency and consistency properties follow from those of\n   413\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\}$ established in Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}\n   414\t\ttogether with Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, which shows that\n   415\t\tcoarse-graining the outcomes preserves the $\\delta_Q$ bounds.\n   416\t\\end{proof}\n   417\t\n   418\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined line measurement on the extended coordinates]\n   419\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-13}\n   420\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-12, def:line, def:line-point-dist, def:consistency,\n   421\t\t\tdef:bracket, def:expanded-state}\n   422\t\t% The source states delta_combine(eps,m,d,q) = poly(m^2 eps, md/q)\n   423\t\t% (references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex,\n   424\t\t% lem:qld-4-13); neither of its two proof routes delivers that form,\n   425\t\t% and the route retained here yields m * poly(eps, md/q). See\n   426\t\t% rem:qld-4-13-source-defects. The divisibility hypothesis is required\n   427\t\t% for the line-point distribution over F_q^{2m+2}; see\n   428\t\t% rem:qld-4-7-divisibility.\n   429\t\tThere exists a function\n   430\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = m \\cdot \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$\n   431\t\tsuch that the following holds. Assume that $2m+2$ divides $q$. For\n   432\t\tevery line $\\ell$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and\n   433\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ there exists a\n   434\t\tPOVM $\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\\}$ acting on $\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$ with\n   435\t\toutcomes $f \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$ such that on average over a uniformly\n   436\t\trandom pair $(\\ell, (x,z,\\alpha,\\beta))$ drawn from the line-point\n   437\t\tdistribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n   438\t\t\\begin{gather}\n   439\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-13}\n   440\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}\n   441\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot)=a]}\n   442\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   443\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}}\n   444\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   445\t\t\t\\\\\n   446\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}\n   447\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot)=a]}\n   448\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{B}'}\n   449\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}}\n   450\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}''}\\,,\n   451\t\t\t\\notag\n   452\t\t\\end{gather}\n   453\t\twhere the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$. Moreover, if $\\ell$ is\n   454\t\taxis-parallel, then the outcome $f \\in \\deg_{d}(\\ell)$.\n   455\t\\end{lemma}\n   720\t\tgiven there lists only two numbered properties, so the reference is\n   721\t\tdangling. The intended assertion is the axis-parallel closure property\n   722\t\trecorded as Property~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} above, which is\n   723\t\timmediate from the sampling procedure in the proof.\n   724\t\\end{remark}\n   725\t\n   726\tThe proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} rests on three scalar estimates, stated\n   727\tnext. In all three, $2m+2$ is assumed to divide $q$, $D$ is the distribution\n   728\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines},\n   729\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}$ is the measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} placed\n   730\ton registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$, the second tensor factor is placed on\n   731\tregisters $\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, and the sums range over\n   732\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$.\n   733\t\n   734\t\\begin{lemma}[Replacing the combined point measurement by the ordered product]\n   735\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-1}\n   736\t\t% claim:17-1 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   737\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity.\n   738\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10,\n   739\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state}\n   740\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   741\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   742\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   743\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   744\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   745\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   746\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\\\\n   747\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q^{1/2}}\\\n   748\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   749\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   750\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   751\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   752\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\,\n   753\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{f_X(x)}\n   754\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   755\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,.\n   756\t\t\\end{align*}\n   757\t\\end{lemma}\n   758\t\\begin{proof}\n   759\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   760\t\t\tdef:line-point-dist}\n   761\t\tWrite\n   762\t\t$W^{x,z}_{a,b} = \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\n   763\t\t- \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b}\n   764\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}$\n   765\t\tand apply the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality to the difference of the two\n   766\t\tsides. The first factor is at most $1$ because\n   767\t\t$\\sum_{f_X,f_Z \\,:\\, f_X(x)=a,\\ f_Z(z)=b}\n   768\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z} \\le \\Id$\n   769\t\tfor every $x, z, a, b$. For the second factor, note that when $\\ell$ is\n   770\t\tdrawn from the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$, a uniformly\n   771\t\trandom point $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ on $\\ell$ is a uniformly random point\n   772\t\tof $\\F_q^{2m+2}$, so the coordinates $x, z$ are independent and uniform\n   773\t\tin $\\F_q^m$; hence\n   774\t\t$\\E_{x,z} \\sum_{a,b}\n   775\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\Id \\ot (W^{x,z}_{a,b})^2 \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   776\t\t\\le \\delta_Q$\n   777\t\tby \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx}. The total error is therefore at most\n   778\t\t$\\sqrt{\\delta_Q}$.\n   779\t\\end{proof}\n   780\t\n   781\t\\begin{lemma}[Removing the $X$-point factor]\n   782\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-2}\n   783\t\t% claim:17-2 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   784\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity. The source right-hand side\n   785\t\t% carries a typographical superscript \"Z\" in place of the point \"z\";\n   786\t\t% the corrected form is stated here.\n   787\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   788\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, lem:qld-comm-line-cons,\n   789\t\t\tdef:expanded-state}\n   790\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   791\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   792\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   793\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   794\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   795\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\,\n   796\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{f_X(x)}\n   797\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   798\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\\\\n   799\t\t\t\\approx_{m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}}\\\n   800\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   801\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   802\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   803\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   804\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\n   805\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   806\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,,\n   807\t\t\\end{align*}\n   808\t\twhere $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}$ is the error function of\n   809\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}.\n   810\t\\end{lemma}\n   811\t\\begin{proof}\n   812\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-comm-line-cons, lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   813\t\t\tlem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds, def:ith-restricted-line,\n   814\t\t\tdef:expanded-line-measurement}\n   815\t\tBy Cauchy--Schwarz, the difference of the two sides is bounded by the\n   816\t\tsquare root of the deficit\n   817\t\t\\[\n   818\t\t\t1 - \\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   819\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell} \\sum_{a}\n   820\t\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   821\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,x}_{a} \\ot\n   822\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   823\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,,\n   824\t\t\t\\qquad\n   825\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,x}_{a}\n   826\t\t\t= \\sum_{f_X,f_Z \\,:\\, f_X(x)=a}\n   827\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\,.\n   828\t\t\\]\n   829\t\tBy the sandwich definition of $T$ and the projectivity of the lines\n   830\t\tmeasurement $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{f_X}\\}$, the\n   831\t\tsubtracted expectation equals\n   832\t\t$\\E \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   833\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a]} \\ot\n   834\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   835\t\twhich by Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} is a mixture of the\n   836\t\tcorresponding averages over the restricted distributions\n   837\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v},i}$. By the error inflation of\n   838\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds} applied to the\n   839\t\tline--point consistency in item~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons},\n   840\t\teach restricted term has deficit $O(m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$, so the\n   841\t\ttotal deficit is $O(m^2\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$ (the final line of the\n   842\t\tsource proof asserts this bound for the consistency term itself rather\n   843\t\tthan for its deficit; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}).\n   844\t\tTaking the square root gives the claimed error\n   845\t\t$m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}$.\n   846\t\\end{proof}\n   847\t\n   848\t\\begin{lemma}[The remaining $Z$-point term is close to one]\n   849\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-3}\n   850\t\t% claim:17-3 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   851\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity.\n   852\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10,\n   853\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state}\n   854\t\t\\[\n   855\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   856\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   857\t\t\t\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   858\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   859\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\n   860\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   861\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   862\t\t\t\\ \\approx_{\\sqrt{m}\\,(\\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} +\n   863\t\t\t\t\\eps^{1/4})}\\ 1\\,.\n   864\t\t\\]\n   865\t\\end{lemma}\n   866\t\\begin{proof}\n   867\t\t\\uses{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds, lem:qld-sublines,\n   868\t\t\tlem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10, lem:qld-comm-cons,\n   869\t\t\tdef:ith-restricted-line}\n   870\t\tBy Cauchy--Schwarz it suffices to bound the deficit\n   871\t\t$1 - \\E \\sum_b \\bra{\\hat\\psi} T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b} \\ot\n   872\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   873\t\twhere\n   874\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b}\n   875\t\t= \\sum_{f_X} T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,[\\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}$.\n   876\t\tBy Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}, the expectation can be\n   877\t\trewritten over the mixture of product distributions\n   878\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v},i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{v},j}$ with $z$ uniformly random on\n   879\t\t$\\ell_Z$, and, since\n   880\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b}\n   881\t\t= \\E_{x \\in \\ell_X} \\sum_a\n   882\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}\n   883\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a,\\ \\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}$,\n   884\t\tone may additionally average over $x$ uniformly random on $\\ell_X$. Up\n   885\t\tto error $m(\\sqrt{\\delta_P} + \\sqrt{\\delta_Q})$ --- obtained by\n   886\t\tapplying the error inflation of\n   887\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds} to the conclusion of\n   888\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} (transported to the sub-line distribution\n   889\t\tvia Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}) and to the consistency relations of\n   890\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10} --- the resulting quantity is close to\n   891\t\t$\\E \\sum_{a,b} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   892\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   893\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ot\n   894\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   895\t\twhich, since $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\\}$ is a measurement,\n   896\t\tequals\n   897\t\t$\\sum_b \\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ot\n   898\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   899\t\t\\ge 1 - \\sqrt{\\eps}$\n   900\t\tby the self-consistency of the points measurements\n   901\t\t(Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}). The deficit is therefore\n   902\t\t$O(m(\\sqrt{\\delta_P} + \\sqrt{\\delta_Q}) + \\sqrt{\\eps})$, and taking the\n   903\t\tsquare root gives the claim.\n   904\t\\end{proof}\n   905\t\n   906\t\\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}]\n   907\t\t\\proves{lem:qld-4-13}\n   908\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-sublines, def:combine-map,\n   909\t\t\tlem:qld-4-12, lem:claim-17-1, lem:claim-17-2, lem:claim-17-3}\n   910\t\tLet $D$ be the distribution of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} and let\n   911\t\t$D_{|\\ell}$ denote $D$ conditioned on the first element being $\\ell$.\n   912\t\tFor every line $\\ell$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and $f \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$\n   913\t\tdefine\n   914\t\t\\[\n   915\t\t\t\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\n   916\t\t\t= \\E_{(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D_{|\\ell}}\n   917\t\t\t\\sum_{\\substack{f_X \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X),\\\n   918\t\t\t\tf_Z \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z):\\\\\n   919\t\t\t\tf = \\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}}}\n   920\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\,,\n   921\t\t\\]\n   922\t\ta valid POVM; the polynomial $\\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ of\n   923\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:combine-map} is well defined here because Property~2\n   924\t\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} guarantees that $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$\n   925\t\tare compatible with $\\ell$. If $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, then by\n   926\t\tProperty~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} so are $\\ell_X$ and\n   927\t\t$\\ell_Z$, hence $f_X, f_Z \\in \\deg_d$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines};\n   928\t\talong such a line either $(\\alpha,\\beta)$ is constant, in which case\n   929\t\t$f = \\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ is a fixed linear combination of\n   930\t\tpolynomials of degree at most $d$, or $(x,z)$ is constant, in which case\n   931\t\t$f$ is affine in the line parameter, so in either case\n   932\t\t$f \\in \\deg_d(\\ell)$. Chaining Lemmas~\\ref{lem:claim-17-1},\n   933\t\t\\ref{lem:claim-17-2} and~\\ref{lem:claim-17-3} with the triangle\n   934\t\tinequality for scalars gives the consistency bound\n   935\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   936\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}\n   937\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   938\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   939\t\t\t\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   940\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   941\t\t\t\\big(\\Id - \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}\n   942\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   943\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   944\t\t\t= O\\big(m\\,(\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4}\n   945\t\t\t+ \\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2})\\big)\\,;\n   946\t\t\\end{equation}\n   947\t\tsince\n   948\t\t$\\delta_P = \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$, $\\delta_Q = \\poly(\\eps)$, and\n   949\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}} = \\poly(\\eps)$, the right-hand side is a\n   950\t\tfunction of the form $m \\cdot \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$, and\n   951\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}$ is taken to be such a function dominating\n   952\t\tit. (The source states a second bound for the same quantity, whose\n   953\t\tderivation is not supported by the properties proved for the sub-line\n   954\t\tdistribution; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}.) Now\n   955\t\tif the pair $(f_X, f_Z)$ evaluates to $(a, b)$ at $(x, z)$, then\n   956\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ evaluates to $\\alpha a + \\beta b$ at\n   957\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta)$; hence the consistency of $T$ with\n   958\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}$ along the sub-line pairs expressed by\n   959\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}, the definition of\n   960\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}$ as the image of $T$ under the combining map, and the\n   961\t\tconstruction of $\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}$ in\n   962\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-12} as the corresponding coarse-graining of\n   963\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}$ together yield the first relation of\n   964\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-4-13}. The second relation follows from the analogous\n   965\t\targument with the registers swapped.\n   966\t\\end{proof}\n   967\t\n   968\t\\begin{remark}[Two error routes in the source proof of\n   969\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}]\n   970\t\t\\label{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}\n   971\t\tThe source proof in \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar} states two different bounds for\n   972\t\tthe quantity \\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}:\n   973\t\t$O(m(\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} +\n   974\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}))$, obtained by chaining the three claims\n   975\t\t(Lemmas~\\ref{lem:claim-17-1}--\\ref{lem:claim-17-3}), and\n   976\t\t$O(m^2 \\delta_P)$, obtained by decomposing the left-hand side of\n   977\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency} into restricted-distribution\n   978\t\tterms and bounding each by \\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}; a\n   979\t\tsentence is also duplicated at the corresponding point of the source,\n   980\t\tthe second copy with a malformed reference. The second route requires\n   981\t\tthe joint law of the pair $((\\ell_X, x), (\\ell_Z, z))$, for\n   982\t\t$(\\ell, \\ell_X, \\ell_Z) \\sim D$ and $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ uniform on\n   983\t\t$\\ell$, to be a mixture of the product distributions\n   984\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v}_1,i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{v}_2,j}$, since\n   985\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} averages over two independently\n   986\t\tsampled line-point pairs; Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}\n   987\t\tdetermines only the two marginal laws of $(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, x)$ and of\n   988\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, z)$ separately, not the joint law of\n   989\t\t$((\\ell_X, x), (\\ell_Z, z))$, so the decomposition invoked by the\n   990\t\tsource is not established there. The proof above therefore uses only\n   991\t\tthe first route. Neither route, moreover, delivers the error form\n   992\t\t$\\poly(m^2\\eps, md/q)$ claimed in the source. The first route proves\n   993\t\tthe bound $O(m(\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} +\n   994\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}))$, a function of the form\n   995\t\t$m \\cdot \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$, and no function of $m^2\\eps$ and $md/q$\n   996\t\talone that vanishes as its arguments vanish dominates it: at\n   997\t\t$\\eps = m^{-4}$, and with $q$ taken large, both $m^2\\eps$ and $md/q$\n   998\t\ttend to $0$ while the term $m\\,\\eps^{1/4} = 1$ does not, so the\n   999\t\tderivation does not support the claimed form. Even granting the\n  1000\t\tdecomposition required by the second route, the bound obtained there\n  1001\t\tis $O(m^2\\delta_P) = m^2 \\cdot \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$, which again carries\n  1002\t\ta separate prefactor polynomial in $m$. The statement of\n  1003\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} above accordingly records the error as\n  1004\t\t$m \\cdot \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$; this form suffices for\n  1005\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, whose error function absorbs the prefactor\n  1006\t\t$m$ into $(md)^a$. In addition, the final line of\n  1007\t\tthe source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:claim-17-2} asserts the bound\n  1008\t\t$O(m^2\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$ for a consistency term that is close to\n  1009\t\tone rather than for its deficit; the statements and sketches above\n  1010\t\trecord the corrected assertions.\n  1011\t\\end{remark}\n  1012\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/0001/cf4cfa6eb26ad593c4e130d033b54e6a455a1ae1/diff.patch:5194:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_101","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\\\\\\proves\\\\{thm:pauli\\\\}|lem:qld-4-7|2m\\\\+2' blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:11:We use the following objects from the preceding chapters: the combined point projectors $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ acting on the expanded space (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}); the projective polynomial-pair measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, with outcomes pairs of polynomials in $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ and error function\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:28:\tThe source states the constructions of this chapter in terms of its boolean decoding map (the case $H = \\{0,1\\}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:decoding-map}) while describing that map as returning the string of \\emph{all} evaluations of $g$ on the hypercube. The proofs require the latter reading, $H = \\F_q$, which is adopted throughout this chapter: the linearity of $\\mathrm{Dec}$ and the identity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for all $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$, both of which fail for the boolean map, are used in the symmetry step of the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} and in the conjugation identity \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}. The source further asserts the identity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)$ for every $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. Since the left-hand side equals $g_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}(u)$, the identity holds exactly when $g$ is an encoding, and fails otherwise: for $m = 1$, $d \\geq 2$, and $q > 2$, the polynomial $g(x) = x^2$ has $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_1(u) = u \\neq u^2$. Accordingly, the proofs below invoke the identity only for encodings; in the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} the contribution of the remaining outcomes is controlled by the bound \\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} on the mass that the measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} places on polynomials that are not encodings, while in the conjugation identity \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the quantity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:36:\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:37:\tLet $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ be the projective measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}. For every $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ define\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:76:\t\\uses{def:tilde-w-observables, def:s-w-marginals, def:generalized-pauli, def:decoding-map, lem:qld-4-7}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:90:\t\\uses{def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:s-w-marginals, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, lem:twisted-commutation, lem:qld-4-7}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:91:\tExpanding $\\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\sum_{h} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\tilde{u} \\cdot h))}\\, \\tau^W_h$ on the right-hand side of \\eqref{eq:tildewj-product-form} (Lemma~\\ref{lem:pauli-observable-expansion}), grouping the $h$ according to the value $a' = h \\cdot \\tilde{u}$ into the projectors $\\tau^W_{a'}(\\tilde{u})$ of \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu}, collecting the pairs $(g_X,g_Z)$ with a common value $g_W = g$ into $\\hat{S}^W_g$ (Definition~\\ref{def:s-w-marginals}), and changing the summation variable to $a = (\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a'$ recovers, by \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tildewj}, the operator $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$; this proves \\eqref{eq:tildewj-product-form}. Since the $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$ are mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}) and $\\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$ is a Hermitian involution, the product form exhib