ed\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:172:% Paper labels sec:finite-fields, sec:subfields, sec:ff-representations kept\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:217:\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:315:% Paper label sec:ld-encoding kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:398:\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:415:\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:494:\tthat $u_0 = u$; the source leaves this case implicit.)\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:498:% Paper label sec:lin-reg kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:529:% Paper label sec:generalized-pauli kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:590:\t\t      % Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:644:\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:737:\\begin{remark}\\label{rem:pauli-binary-source}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:738:\tIn the source statement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pauli-binary} in\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:3:% Paper label sec:pauli-verifier kept for cross-reference fidelity; it resolves to this chapter.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:11:% Paper labels sec:ld-verifier and sec:ld-game kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:19:\t% The source fixes \\N to be the set of positive integers\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:20:\t% (references/qpbt-paper/04_preliminaries.tex:6) and takes m, d, ldc in \\N\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:21:\t% (references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:33-35),\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:22:\t% so the positivity below is the source's implicit domain, stated explicitly.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:26:\t% hold trivially, is likewise outside the source's domain and is omitted.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:97:\t\t% The source phrases both clauses with \"the\" t such that x = u_0 + tv; that phrase\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:98:\t\t% does not determine t on diagonal questions with direction v' = 0, which the\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:100:\t\t% quantification over t used here is the local correction; see the remark below.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:108:\tThe source states the two line-versus-point clauses with ``the'' $t \\in \\F_q$ such that $x_{\\overline w} = u_0 + t v$. When the direction is nonzero and $x_{\\overline w}$ lies on the line, exactly one such $t$ exists and the formulation above agrees with the source. But zero directions occur: Definition~\\ref{def:line} explicitly permits them, and the question distribution of Definition~\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} produces $\\mathsf{DLine}$ questions with $v' = \\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$ with positive probability, namely whenever the last $m - i + 1$ coordinates of $v$ vanish. In that case $\\ell(u_0, 0) = \\{u_0\\}$, every $t \\in \\F_q$ satisfies $x_{\\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$ when $x_{\\overline w} = u_0$, and the source clause does not determine acceptance for a nonconstant answer polynomial. The universal quantification over $t$ adopted in Definition~\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} makes the predicate a well-defined function of its arguments; it accepts vacuously when no such $t$ exists, a case the question distribution never produces. Completeness is unaffected: the restriction of a polynomial $g$ to the singleton line $\\ell(u_0,0)$, expressed through the parameterization $t \\mapsto u_0 + t \\cdot 0$, is the constant polynomial with value $g(u_0)$, which passes the check for every $t$. Soundness is likewise unaffected: zero directions occur with probability at most $2/(mq)$, and the proof of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} shows that any acceptance convention on this event changes success probabilities by at most that amount, which is absorbed into the universal constants of $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:115:\tLet $\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ denote the set of POVM measurements indexed by $\\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$, the polynomial representatives of individual degree at most $d$ in each variable; this is the notion of Definition~\\ref{def:polymeasurements}, and the argument order $(m,q,d)$ follows that definition (the paper orders the arguments as $(m,d,q)$). By Definition~\\ref{def:polynomials-degree}, evaluation identifies these representatives with the corresponding polynomial functions $g\\colon \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$, bijectively whenever $d \\le q-1$; the parameter regimes of this chapter (Definition~\\ref{def:introparams} fixes $d = 1$, and admissibility gives $q \\ge 2$) always satisfy this. More generally, for an integer $k$ and tuples $m = (m_1,\\ldots,m_k)$, $q = (q_1,\\ldots,q_k)$, $d = (d_1,\\ldots,d_k)$, let $\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$ denote the set of measurements $G = \\{G_{g_1,\\ldots,g_k}\\}$ such that for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, the outcome index $g_i$ is a polynomial $g_i\\colon \\F_{q_i}^{m_i} \\to \\F_{q_i}$ of individual degree at most $d_i$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:118:Quantum soundness of a version of the classical low-degree test for polynomials of low \\emph{total} degree was claimed by Natarajan and Vidick, building on an analysis of a three-prover version of the test by Vidick; there is a known gap in the soundness analysis of that test, and it is not used here. Instead, the construction relies on the low \\emph{individual} degree test, whose quantum soundness is proved in~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} and generalized in~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}. The statement below is the form most directly useful in what follows.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:120:% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:149:\t% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:151:\t% q-d of the degree-d evaluation code, and the dimension claim d+1 requires d < q.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:155:\t% Definition def:ld-game, following the source's convention that \\N denotes\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:157:\t% The source asserts a flat identity between the two tests\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:158:\t% (references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:446-454).\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:159:\t% After the zero-direction correction of Definition def:ld-win-predicate\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:161:\t% zero-direction diagonal questions, so the import is scoped to the predicate\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:162:\t% analyzed in the external source and bridged by the probability estimate below,\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:164:\tThe two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\\code^{\\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, agrees with the game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$ on every question pair whose diagonal-line direction is nonzero: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. On diagonal line-versus-point questions with direction $v' = \\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$, the win predicate of Definition~\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} resolves an ambiguity of the source (Remark~\\ref{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}), and the resolution is not required to match the acceptance convention of the subcube commutation test on this event: the event has probability at most $2/(mq)$ under the question distribution, since the direction $\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ vanishes with probability $\\frac1m \\sum_{j=1}^m q^{-j} \\le \\frac{1}{m(q-1)} \\le \\frac{2}{mq}$ over uniform $(s,v)$, so the success probabilities of any fixed strategy in the two games differ by at most $2/(mq)$. A projective strategy that succeeds in $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ with probability at least $1-\\eps$ therefore succeeds in the tensor-code test with probability at least $1 - \\eps - 2/(mq)$. Theorem~4.7 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\\mathsf{ldparams}$) and applied with the success deficit $\\eps + 2/(mq)$ in place of $\\eps$, then yields the present statement for $k = 1$: since $0 < b \\le 1$ and $m \\ge 1$ give $(\\eps + 2/(mq))^b \\le \\eps^b + 2\\,q^{-b}$, the additional error is absorbed into $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}$ by enlarging the universal constant $a$, using $dmk \\ge 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:168:\tTheorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is imported: the proof above reduces it to Theorem~4.7 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$, followed by the extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples of~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The result of~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} that underlies it is proved here as Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}: for every projective strategy passing the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test of Definition~\\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} with probability at least $1-\\eps$, that theorem produces measurements in $\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ satisfying consistency relations of the same shape as the three approximations of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is not a consequence of Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal} alone: a derivation from it would require, in addition, an equivalence between the typed game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{(q,m,d,1)}$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\\ref{chap:test} --- matching the two question distributions, the two win conditions, and the two error functions --- together with the extension from $k = 1$ to general $k$ by the reduction of~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} cited above. Neither implication is proved in this chapter, and the proof of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} given above does not invoke Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:172:% Paper label sec:ms kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:253:% Paper label sec:qld-game kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:260:\t% The source introduces q, m, d in \\N with \\N the positive integers\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:261:\t% (references/qpbt-paper/04_preliminaries.tex:6 and\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:262:\t% references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:903-905),\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:268:\t% whose domain (Definition def:ld-game) requires d >= 1. The hypothesis\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:277:\t% the source parametrizes the Pauli basis test by the tuple qldparams introduced\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:278:\t% together with Definition def:admissible (references/qpbt-paper/\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:310:\t% def:graph-distribution, matching the source's formal definition\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:311:\t% (references/qpbt-paper/07_types.tex:84-93 together with\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:312:\t% references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:1115-1120).\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:323:\t% The paper's format table writes the line contents loosely as (u_W, s) and (u_W, s, v);\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:384:\t\t% The paper here has the typo \"phi_alice \\ot phi_B\"; both maps are the isometries above.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:404:\t% The paper's displayed function here writes the argument order (eps, q, m, d); the\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:427:\tThe paper writes the soundness function of the Pauli basis test with two different argument orders: $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps, m, d, q)$ in Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:delta-bound}, and $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps, q, m, d)$ in the displayed function of Corollary~\\ref{cor:pauli-binary}, whose own item~1 reverts to $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps, m, d, q)$; the formula is the same throughout, and here the order $(\\eps, m, d, q)$ is used everywhere. Note also two deliberate asymmetries with the classical soundness function of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}: there the polynomial prefactor is $a(dmk)^a$ and the exponent satisfies $0 < b \\le 1$, while in Theorems~\\ref{thm:pauli} and Corollary~\\ref{cor:pauli-binary} the prefactor is $a(md)^a$ and the exponent satisfies the strict inequality $0 < b < 1$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:431:% Paper label sec:qld-complexity kept for cross-reference fidelity (the complexity\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:432:% statements of that subsection are outside the scope of this development).\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:469:\\begin{remark}[A comparison of exponents in the source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:delta-bound}]\\label{rem:delta-bound-exponent-comparison}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:470:\tThe source proof bounds the third term of $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$ by asserting $2^{-bmd} \\le q^{-b}$ ``because $k \\le m$'', comparing the field exponent $k = c \\lceil \\log\\log R \\rceil + 1$ with $m = 2^j$. The inequality $k \\le m$ does not follow from Definition~\\ref{def:introparams}: if the universal constants of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} were $a = 1$ and $b = 1/2$, then $c = 4$, and $R = 5$ gives $k = 9$ and $m = 8$. The proof above instead bounds $2^{-bm} \\le R^{-b}$ directly from $m \\ge (c/2) \\log R \\ge \\log R$, which yields the same conclusion after enlarging the universal constant $a'$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:3:% Paper label sec:games kept for cross-reference fidelity; resolves to the chapter number.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:12:\t% Finiteness of the outcome set is stated explicitly here; the source leaves it implicit,\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:34:% the special case where f is a coordinate projection. Verified against the Lean source\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:53:\tThe symmetrization argument of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} below invokes Naimark dilation in the form of Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark} (stated and proved in Chapter~\\ref{chap:projective}): a bipartite state together with two sub-measurement families can be replaced, without changing any of the correlations $\\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^y_b \\ket{\\psi}$, by a product-extended state together with projective sub-measurements on the enlarged spaces. When the input families are complete measurements, the dilated projective sub-measurements can be completed by assigning the residual projector of each family to one fixed outcome; the resulting projective measurements reproduce the same correlations on the extended state, since the correlation-preservation identity forces the extended state to lie in the range of the total projector of each dilated family. This completed form, with genuinely projective measurements, is the one used in Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat}; for the proof the source refers to Theorem 5.1 of \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}, which is stated for complete measurements. The source follows its statement of the dilation theorem with a sentence announcing an ``orthogonalization lemma'' from the literature that is never actually stated there; nothing in this chapter depends on it.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:57:% Paper subsection label sec:strat kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:95:% The source labels this definition def:projective-strategy. That label is already carried\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:108:\t% Paper label: rem:symmetric-games (a definition environment carrying a rem: label in the\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:109:\t% source); relabeled here to match the environment kind.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:118:% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:94-103) concludes with a\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:139:\tThe source states the conclusion of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} in the stronger form: there exists a symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\\eps$. Its proof, reproduced above, produces, for each $\\eps' > \\eps$, such a strategy of value at least $1-\\eps'$, and the stronger form does not follow: the dilated dimension $d$ may grow as $\\eps' \\downarrow \\eps$, and the supremum defining $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:tensor-product-value} need not be attained by any strategy. The statement above therefore records the approximate conclusion, which is what the proof establishes; the stronger form would require an additional attainment or compactness argument.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:237:\tStated in the source without proof; these are Facts 4.13 and 4.14 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For the first item, expand each term $\\lVert (A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$ and use $ (A^x_a)^2 \\le A^x_a$, $(B^x_a)^2 \\le B^x_a$ together with completeness of the two POVMs: summing over $a$ and averaging over $x$ leaves exactly twice the off-diagonal mass, which is $O(\\delta)$ by hypothesis. For the converse items, write the off-diagonal mass $\\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi}$ as an inner product involving $(A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi}$ and use projectivity of both families, or of one family combined with the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality; the latter route costs a square root in the error.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:264:\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:347-361) ends with\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:266:\t% quantity by O(delta), which is what the conclusion requires.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:285:\t% O(delta), not by delta itself; the source\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:286:\t% (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:364-380) elides this.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:296:\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.28 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For every $x$ and $a$, $\\lVert (A^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 \\le 2\\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 + 2\\lVert (B^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$. Averaging over $x$ and summing over $a$ gives the claim; the factor $2$ is absorbed by the $O(\\cdot)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:308:\tStated in the source without proof; it is Proposition 4.29 of \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}. The two hypotheses involving $C$ convert, via Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, into state-dependent closeness of $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B$ to both $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ and $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$, hence of $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ to $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$ at cost $O(\\delta+\\gamma)$. The first consistency then transfers from $B$ to $D$ by expanding the off-diagonal mass and applying the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, which turns the squared-distance bound into the term $2\\sqrt{\\delta+\\gamma}$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:319:\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.26 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Every off-diagonal pair $(b, b')$ with $b \\ne b'$ of the post-processed families pulls back to pairs $(a, a')$ with $f(a) = b \\ne b' = f(a')$, and in particular $a \\ne a'$. Coarse-graining therefore only merges terms of the off-diagonal sum, so the post-processed off-diagonal mass is at most the original one.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:357:% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:465-495) leaves the\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:361:% NEEXP original (references/neexp-paper/05_quantum_preliminaries.tex:952-973, label\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:372:\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:465-495) prints the\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:375:\t% source. The empty product for k = 0 is also made explicit here. See\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:387:\tThe source proof is a citation: the argument is identical to the proof of Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, with the only modification being the insertion of the dependence on the question $x$ for all measurements considered. In outline, the consistency hypotheses \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-1} allow one to insert the projective factors $G^{i,\\,x}$ next to $A^x$ one index at a time, and the $\\eps$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\mathcal G_i$ controls the events on which two different outcomes collide at the sampled point $y$. Each of the $k$ insertions costs $(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}$ by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, producing the sandwich $C^x$ with total error $k(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:391:\tAs printed in the source, the sandwich in Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich} pairs the $i$-th measurement with the outcome $g_{k+1-i}$: the outermost factor $G^{k,\\,x}$ carries the outcome $g_1$ and the innermost factor $G^{1,\\,x}$ carries $g_k$. Since the measurement $\\{G^{i,\\,x}_g\\}$ is indexed by $g \\in \\mathcal G_i$, the operator $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_1}$ is undefined whenever $\\mathcal G_1 \\ne \\mathcal G_k$, so the printed pairing is a typographical slip. The statement above instead uses the pairing $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} \\cdots G^{1,\\,x}_{g_1} \\cdots G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k}$ of Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, from which the lemma is imported. The source also leaves the case $k = 0$ implicit; with the empty-product convention recorded in the statement, both sides of \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-2} are then the identity on the single empty-tuple outcome and the conclusion holds with error zero.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:416:\tNeither the exponent nor the constant in $\\poly(\\eta, \\delta)$ is made explicit in the source; both are universal constants inherited from the proof of Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:419:\tStated in the source without proof, as an import of Fact 4.35 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The measurement $J^x$ sandwiches the family $(G_1)^x$ between two copies of the projective family $(G_2)^x$. The consistency hypotheses \\eqref{eq:pasting-1} and the self-consistency \\eqref{eq:pasting-2} allow the joint measurement $A^{x,y_1,y_2}$ to be compared with the sandwiched product one factor at a time, and the $\\eta$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\mathcal G_2$ controls the error terms arising when two different outcomes $g_2 \\ne g_2'$ agree at the sampled point $y_2$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:430:\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.32 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Write the value difference as an average over $(x,y) \\sim \\mu$ of sums of terms of the form $\\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - (A')^x_a) \\ot B^y_b \\ket{\\psi}$ and $\\bra{\\psi} (A')^x_a \\ot (B^y_b - (B')^y_b) \\ket{\\psi}$, replacing one player's measurements at a time. The Cauchy--Schwarz inequality bounds each group by the square root of the corresponding averaged squared state-dependent distance, and projectivity of one of the two strategies keeps the accompanying operator sums bounded, giving the total bound $O(\\delta^{1/2})$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:434:% Paper subsection label sec:cl kept for cross-reference fidelity.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:525:% Paper section label sec:types kept for cross-reference fidelity. Only the mathematical\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:527:% here; the typed sampler and verifier machinery of the source is outside the scope of\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:539:% normalization 1/(2m-k) below is undefined. The source\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:540:% (references/qpbt-paper/07_types.tex:65-82) leaves this boundary hypothesis implicit.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:554:% needed for the graph distribution mu_G to be defined; the source\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:555:% (references/qpbt-paper/07_types.tex:84-93) leaves it implicit here as well.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/0001/cf4cfa6eb26ad593c4e130d033b54e6a455a1ae1/diff.patch:5239:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_125","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '95,278p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '1410,1880p'\"","aggregated_output":"    95\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex): the source asserts commutation\n    96\t% whenever j != j'.\n    97\t\\begin{remark}[The commutation phase across basis indices]\\label{rem:qld-cross-phase}\n    98\t\tFor $j \\neq j'$ the source asserts that $\\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u})$ and $\\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v})$ commute for all $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v} \\in \\F_q^M$. Since $e_j e_{j'} \\neq 0$ and the bilinear form $(a,b) \\mapsto \\tr(ab)$ on $\\F_q$ is nondegenerate, there exist $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v}$ with $\\tr(e_j e_{j'} (\\tilde{u} \\cdot \\tilde{v})) = 1$, and for these \\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} yields anticommutation instead. By self-duality of the basis $\\{e_j\\}$, the dichotomy asserted by the source --- commutation for $j \\neq j'$, and the phase $(-1)^{\\tr((e_j\\tilde{u}) \\cdot (e_j\\tilde{v}))}$ for $j = j'$ --- agrees with \\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} whenever $\\tilde{u} \\cdot \\tilde{v} \\in \\{0,1\\}$, in particular for all $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v} \\in \\{0,1\\}^M$. The relations \\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} are not used in the remainder of the chapter.\n    99\t\\end{remark}\n   100\t\n   101\t\\section{Consistency with the points measurements}\n   102\t\n   103\tThe next lemma shows that the pulled-apart measurements are consistent with the original points measurements of the strategy, and self-consistent. In its statement, and for the remainder of the chapter, $\\delta_S$ denotes the function of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, enlarged by an adjustment of the universal constants $a$ and $b$ so as to also dominate additional error terms of order $\\sqrt{\\eps}$, $\\eps$, and $md/q$ that the source absorbs into $\\delta_S$ along the way; such an adjustment is possible since, after shrinking $b$ to at most $1/2$, the term $\\eps^b$ dominates $\\sqrt{\\eps}$ and $\\eps$ for $\\eps \\leq 1$, while $(md)^a q^{-b}$ dominates $md/q$.\n   104\t\n   105\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency and self-consistency of the pulled-apart measurements]\\label{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}\n   106\t\t\\uses{def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tilde-w-observables, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:consistency, def:povm-distance, lem:qld-4-7, def:indicator-vector}\n   107\t\tThe measurements $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ and observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ satisfy the following properties.\n   108\t\t% The paper's statement also quantifies item 1 over j, but j does not occur\n   109\t\t% in item 1; the vacuous quantifier is dropped here.\n   110\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   111\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   112\t\t\t\\[\n   113\t\t\t\t\\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\simeq_{\\delta_S} M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}\n   114\t\t\t\\]\n   115\t\t\tand\n   116\t\t\t\\[\n   117\t\t\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} \\simeq_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a\\;,\n   118\t\t\t\\]\n   119\t\t\twith the answer summation over $a \\in \\F_q$.\n   120\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, on average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^{M}$,\n   121\t\t\t\\[\n   122\t\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})\\;.\n   123\t\t\t\\]\n   124\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   125\t\\end{lemma}\n   126\t\\begin{proof}\n   127\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:decoding-map, def:indicator-vector, lem:qld-win-implications, lem:qld-comm-cons, fact:add-a-proj, fact:agreement, fact:triangle, fact:data-processing, def:bracket, lem:povm-to-obs, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}\n   128\t\t% Support restriction added: the source (line 1492 of\n   129\t\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex) rewrites Dec(g).ind_m(u) as\n   130\t\t% g(u) for every outcome g; see Remark rem:qld-decoding-identity.\n   131\t\tWe first bound the mass that the marginal measurements $\\{\\hat{S}^W_g\\}$ place on polynomials that are not encodings. On average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$, the point-consistency relation \\eqref{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob} of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} gives $(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''}$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}); the consistency and Pauli-basis-consistency checks of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} give, again on average over uniform $u$ and combining through Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, and Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle}, that $(M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{a'})_{\\mathrm{A}} \\approx_{O(\\eps)} (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a']})_{\\mathrm{A}}$; and replacing the point factor of the convolution form of $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}) accordingly costs the same error, because the projectors $\\tau^W_{a''}(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u))$ of \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} are mutually orthogonal (Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}). Writing\n   132\t\t\\[\n   133\t\t\tK^u_a = \\sum_{a' + a'' = a} \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a']}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\bigl(\\tau^W_{a''}(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u))\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   134\t\t\\]\n   135\t\ta projective measurement, Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle} and Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} therefore give, using $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$,\n   136\t\t\\[\n   137\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (K^u_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\geq 1 - O(\\delta_S)\\;.\n   138\t\t\\]\n   139\t\tExpanding $K^u_a$ over pairs $(h, h'')$ of Pauli outcomes, a term of the left-hand side with outcomes $(g, h, h'')$ is constrained by $g(u) = g_h(u) + g_{h''}(u) = g_{h + h''}(u)$, by the linearity of the encoding \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}. If $g$ is not an encoding then $g \\neq g_{h+h''}$, and both are polynomials of total degree at most $md$, so the constraint holds for at most an $md/q$ fraction of $u$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}). Since all terms are nonnegative and, for each $g$, the operators of the $(h,h'')$-sum add up to the identity, it follows that\n   140\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass}\n   141\t\t\t\\sum_{g \\,\\text{not an encoding}} \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S) + \\frac{md}{q} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S)\\;,\n   142\t\t\\end{equation}\n   143\t\tand symmetrically with the registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ in place of $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$. (If $d = 0$, every element of $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ is constant, hence an encoding, and the left-hand side vanishes.)\n   144\t\n   145\t\tFor the first item, in the case where $M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ acts on register $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$ on $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, expanding \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} and using the convolution form of the combined projectors $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}) gives the exact identity\n   146\t\t\\[\n   147\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   148\t\t\t= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_g \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ot (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\;.\n   149\t\t\\]\n   150\t\tFor encodings $g$ the subscript $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ equals $g(u)$; discarding the (nonnegative) terms with $g$ not an encoding and reinstating the corresponding terms with subscript $g(u)$, each of which is at most $\\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$, shows that the right-hand side is at least\n   151\t\t\\[\n   152\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_g \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ot (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} - O(\\delta_S)\n   153\t\t\t\\geq 1 - O(\\delta_S)\n   154\t\t\\]\n   155\t\tby \\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, and the register-interchanged relation follows analogously. For the second item one first shows that, on average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M$,\n   156\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-pulling-cons}\n   157\t\t\t\\bigl( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\bigl( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}\\;:\n   158\t\t\\end{equation}\n   159\t\tright-multiplying by the resolution $\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id$ of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (with Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}) and expanding the products of $\\tau^W$ projectors via \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} and $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_h(u)$ places $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}$ within $O(\\delta_S)$ of a sum over pairs $(g,h)$ with $(\\mathrm{Dec}(g)-h)\\cdot\\tilde{u} = a$. One then applies, in turn, the self-consistency of the points measurements (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}) at cost $\\eps$; the exact identity $\\ket{\\hat\\psi} = \\sum_{h'} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ of the EPR registers; Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} twice more (once through a Cauchy--Schwarz detour of cost $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ using the self-consistency of the $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}); and finally a Schwartz--Zippel step (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}, for polynomials of total degree at most $md$) which replaces the event $(g'-g_{h'})(u) = (g-g_h)(u)$ by the polynomial identity $g'-g_{h'} = g-g_h$ at cost $md/q$. The resulting expression\n   160\t\t\\[\n   161\t\t\t\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h \\\\ (\\mathrm{Dec}(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}}\n   162\t\t\t\\Bigl((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\tau_h^W)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\Bigr) \\cdot \\Bigl( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\Bigr)\n   163\t\t\\]\n   164\t\tis symmetric under exchanging $(g,h)$ with $(g',h')$: since $\\mathrm{Dec}$ is linear with $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_{h}) = h$ for all $h \\in \\F_q^M$ (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}), the constraint $g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h$ implies $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) - h = \\mathrm{Dec}(g') - h'$, so the two constraint sets coincide. The same derivation therefore bounds the distance of the displayed expression to $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$, and \\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons} follows after absorbing the error terms $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ and $O(md/q)$ into $\\delta_S$. Finally, since $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}$ is projective, Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, data processing along the map $a \\mapsto \\tr(e_j a)$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}), and Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} yield $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$ with answer summation over $b \\in \\F_2$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:povm-to-obs} converts this into the claimed self-consistency of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$.\n   165\t\\end{proof}\n   166\t\n   167\t% Following the source, the next lemma is stated after\n   168\t% lem:qld-construct-the-paulis, whose proof invokes it; its own proof\n   169\t% depends only on lem:qld-4-7 and lem:qld-comm-cons, so there is no\n   170\t% circularity.\n   171\tLemmas~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} and~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons} imply the following two estimates, which were used in the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}.\n   172\t\n   173\t\\begin{lemma}[The marginals absorb the point measurements]\\label{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper}\n   174\t\t\\uses{def:s-w-marginals, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:povm-distance, lem:qld-4-7, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   175\t\tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   176\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-sg-cons}\n   177\t\t\t\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id\n   178\t\t\\end{equation}\n   179\t\tand\n   180\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-sg-cons2}\n   181\t\t\t\\bigl(\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{g(u)})\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} 0\\;,\n   182\t\t\\end{equation}\n   183\t\twhere the answer summation is over $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$, and where in \\eqref{eq:qld-sg-cons2} an additional error term of order $\\eps$ is absorbed into $\\delta_S$. Furthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also hold (Remark~\\ref{def:symmetric-equivalents}).\n   184\t\\end{lemma}\n   185\t\\begin{proof}\n   186\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, fact:agreement, fact:add-a-proj, def:bracket, lem:qld-comm-cons}\n   187\t\tThe point-consistency conclusion of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} states that, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   188\t\t\\[\n   189\t\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S\\;,\n   190\t\t\\]\n   191\t\twhich says exactly that the operator on the left-hand side of \\eqref{eq:qld-sg-cons} is $\\delta_S$-consistent with $\\Id$; Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} upgrades this $\\simeq_{\\delta_S}$ statement to the $\\approx_{\\delta_S}$ statement \\eqref{eq:qld-sg-cons}. For \\eqref{eq:qld-sg-cons2}, the same consistency, written with the coarse-graining $\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]} = \\sum_{g : g(u) = a} \\hat{S}^W_g$ as $(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{a})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''}$ with answer summation over $a \\in \\F_q$, is left-multiplied by $(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'}$ using Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}; moving $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_a$ from registers $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''$ to $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ costs a further $\\eps$ by Cauchy--Schwarz together with the self-consistency of the $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_a$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}), giving\n   192\t\t\\[\n   193\t\t\t(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\approx_{O(\\delta_S + \\eps)} (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{a})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'}\\;.\n   194\t\t\\]\n   195\t\tExpanding\n   196\t\t\\[\n   197\t\t\t\\E_u \\sum_g \\bigl\\| (\\hat{S}^W_{g} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{g(u)}))_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\bigr\\|^2\n   198\t\t\t= \\E_u \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\bigl((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{a}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point}, W), u}_{a})\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   199\t\t\\]\n   200\t\tand bounding the right-hand side by $O(\\delta_S + \\eps)$ using the two approximations above yields \\eqref{eq:qld-sg-cons2} after absorbing $\\eps$ into $\\delta_S$. The symmetric equivalents follow by the same argument with the register bipartitions interchanged.\n   201\t\\end{proof}\n   202\t\n   203\t\\section{The swap unitaries and the extraction of the Pauli group}\n   204\t\n   205\t\\begin{definition}[The swap unitaries]\\label{def:v-swap-unitary}\n   206\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, def:generalized-pauli, def:decoding-map}\n   207\t\tDefine the linear map\n   208\t\t\\[\n   209\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\bigl(\\tau^X(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)) \\, \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X))\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}''}\\;,\n   210\t\t\\]\n   211\t\twhere $\\tau^X(\\cdot)$ and $\\tau^Z(\\cdot)$ are the generalized Pauli observables acting on $(\\C^q)^{\\ot M}$; note that the argument of $\\tau^X$ is $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)$ and the argument of $\\tau^Z$ is $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X)$. The map $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$, acting on registers $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, is defined analogously.\n   212\t\\end{definition}\n   213\t\n   214\t% In the source the following properties are established inline with the\n   215\t% definition of the swap unitaries; they are recorded here as a lemma.\n   216\t\\begin{lemma}[Unitarity and conjugation action of the swap unitaries]\\label{lem:v-swap-conjugation}\n   217\t\t\\uses{def:v-swap-unitary, def:tilde-w-observables, def:tilde-m-measurement, def:generalized-pauli, def:indicator-vector, def:bracket}\n   218\t\tThe maps $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:v-swap-unitary} are unitaries acting on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ and $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$ respectively, and their conjugation action is exact, as follows. For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, and $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M$,\n   219\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:v-swap-obs-conjugation}\n   220\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger = \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\bigl( \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}''}\n   221\t\t\t\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n   222\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\, V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger = \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot \\bigl( \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   223\t\t\\end{equation}\n   224\t\tand for all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, $u \\in \\F_q^m$, and $a \\in \\F_q$,\n   225\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-unitary-6}\n   226\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\, V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger = \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_{[g_h(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   227\t\t\t\\qquad\n   228\t\t\t\\tau^W_{[g_h(u)=a]} = \\sum_{h \\,:\\, g_h(u) = a} \\tau^W_h\\;,\n   229\t\t\\end{equation}\n   230\t\ttogether with the analogous identity for $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$.\n   231\t\\end{lemma}\n   232\t\\begin{proof}\n   233\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, lem:tildew-product-form, lem:twisted-commutation, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:indicator-vector, def:decoding-map, def:generalized-pauli}\n   234\t\tSince the measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ is projective (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}) and the $\\tau^X, \\tau^Z$ observables are self-inverse,\n   235\t\t\\[\n   236\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger = \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}''} = \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}\\;,\n   237\t\t\\]\n   238\t\tand the same computation gives $V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger V_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\Id$, so $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ is unitary; likewise $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$. For \\eqref{eq:v-swap-obs-conjugation}, conjugate the product form \\eqref{eq:tildewj-product-form} of $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:tildew-product-form}): by the orthogonality of the projectors $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$, only the diagonal terms survive, and the twisted commutation relation (Lemma~\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation})\n   239\t\t\\[\n   240\t\t\t\\tau^X(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)) \\, \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X)) \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = (-1)^{\\tr( e_j(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\, \\tau^X(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)) \\, \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X))\n   241\t\t\\]\n   242\t\tcancels the sign $(-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))}$ term by term, leaving $\\sum_{g_X,g_Z} \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z} \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\Id \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$. For \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}, expand $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$ by \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu}; by the orthogonality of the $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$, conjugation replaces, in the term of $\\hat{S}^W_g$, each projector $\\tau^W_h$ by $\\tau^W_{h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)}$, using the identities $\\tau^X(s)\\, \\tau^Z_h \\,\\tau^X(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^Z_{h+s}$ and $\\tau^Z(s)\\, \\tau^X_h\\, \\tau^Z(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^X_{h+s}$. Substituting $h' = h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)$ turns the constraint $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = (\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)) - a$ into $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = -a = a$, since $\\F_q$ has characteristic $2$; the quantity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly, so no relation between it and $g(u)$ is used (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}). Since $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_{h'}(u)$ \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}, summing over $g$ gives \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}; the computation for $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ is identical.\n   243\t\\end{proof}\n   244\t\n   245\t\\begin{lemma}[Extraction of the EPR state and the Pauli observables]\\label{lem:qld-unitary}\n   246\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, def:v-swap-unitary, def:expanded-state, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:generalized-pauli, def:EPR, def:povm-distance}\n   247\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$, where $\\delta_S$ is the function of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, and there exist unitaries $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ acting on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ and $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$ acting on registers $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, such that the following hold.\n   248\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   249\t\t\t\\item There exists a state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ such that\n   250\t\t\t\\[\n   251\t\t\t\t\\bigl\\| V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\bigr\\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\\;.\n   252\t\t\t\\]\n   253\t\t\t\\item For $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n   254\t\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   255\t\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''}\\;, \\\\\n   256\t\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   257\t\t\t\\end{align*}\n   258\t\t\twhere the $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''}$, with the answer summation over $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$ and no average over questions.\n   259\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   260\t\tThe unitaries may be taken to be $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:v-swap-unitary}.\n   261\t\\end{lemma}\n   262\t\\begin{proof}\n   263\t\t\\uses{def:v-swap-unitary, lem:v-swap-conjugation, lem:tildew-product-form, lem:qld-construct-the-paulis, lem:qld-win-implications, fact:triangle-for-simeq, def:bracket, lem:cancellation, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree, lem:qld-4-7}\n   264\t\tFor the first item, the self-consistency of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j$ (Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}) is equivalent, since these observables are Hermitian involutions (Lemma~\\ref{lem:tildew-product-form}), to $\\E_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}} \\E_{\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M} \\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ot \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2$; setting $\\ket{\\vartheta} = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ and using the exact conjugation identities of Lemma~\\ref{lem:v-swap-conjugation}, this becomes $\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2$ for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, where $H_W = \\E_j \\E_{\\tilde{u}} \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\bigl( \\E_{s \\in \\F_q} \\tau^W(s) \\ot \\tau^W(s) \\bigr)^{\\ot M}$ by the tensor factorization of $\\tau^W(\\tilde{u})$ and the invariance of the uniform average over $s$ under $s \\mapsto e_j s$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:cancellation}, $\\E_{s,s'} \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') \\ot \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') = \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ on each pair of qudits, whence $H_X H_Z = \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$. The bounds $\\| (\\Id - H_W)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq \\delta_S$ and $\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W^2 \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - 2\\delta_S$, combined through the triangle inequality $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq 2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ (see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}), give $\\bra{\\vartheta} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - 4\\delta_S$.\n   265\t\t% Case distinction added: the source normalizes the projected vector\n   266\t\t% without ensuring that it is nonzero; see Remark rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip.\n   267\t\tIf $\\delta_S > 1/8$, then, after enlarging the universal constant in $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$, both items of the lemma hold with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ an arbitrary unit state, since their left-hand sides never exceed $4$; assume therefore $\\delta_S \\leq 1/8$. Then the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ has squared norm at least $1 - 4\\delta_S \\geq 1/2 > 0$, and $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ may be defined as its normalization; the overlap of $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ equals the norm of that vector and is at least $\\sqrt{1 - 4\\delta_S} \\geq 1 - 4\\delta_S$, which yields the first item with a bound of $8\\delta_S = O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$. For the second item, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} gives, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$, $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\eps \\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ and $\\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\simeq_\\eps M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id$, where $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} = \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = a} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h$; combining these with Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} by Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle-for-simeq}, using $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$, yields $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}} \\Id \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$. Writing $\\ket{\\Delta} = \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$, the quantity to be bounded for the $\\mathrm{A}$-side relation equals $2 - 2\\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\Delta}$, since $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} = (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$. Replacing $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ by the averaged coarse-graining $\\E_u \\sum_{h' : g_{h'}(u) = g_h(u)} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ costs at most $md/q$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree} (distinct $h \\neq h'$ have distinct encodings $g_h \\neq g_{h'}$, of total degree at most $md$), and by \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the resulting sum equals $\\E_u \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger \\ket{\\Delta}$. Exchanging $\\ket{\\Delta}$ for $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ costs $O(\\delta_S^{1/4})$ by the first item, and the resulting expression $\\E_u \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ is at least $1 - O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$ by the $\\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}}$ relation above. Altogether the distance is $O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$; the $\\mathrm{B}$-side relation is proved identically.\n   268\t\\end{proof}\n   269\t\n   270\t\\begin{remark}[Two steps in the source proof of the extraction lemma]\\label{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}\n   271\t\tIn the source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, the triangle inequality gives $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq \\| (\\Id - H_X)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| + \\| (\\Id - H_Z)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq 2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$, so that $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq 4\\delta_S$; the source instead asserts the bound $2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ for the \\emph{squared} norm, which holds only when $\\sqrt{\\delta_S} \\leq 1/2$, and consequently carries the term $\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ into an overlap bound of $1 - 2\\delta_S - \\sqrt{\\delta_S}$. The proof above uses the corrected bound $4\\delta_S$, which gives the overlap bound $1 - 4\\delta_S$ and leaves the final bound $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}} = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ unchanged. The source proof also normalizes the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ without ensuring that it is nonzero; the case distinction on $\\delta_S$ in the proof above supplies this, the conclusions of the lemma being trivial when $\\delta_S$ exceeds a universal constant.\n   272\t\\end{remark}\n   273\t\n   274\t\\section{Proof of the soundness of the Pauli basis test}\n   275\t\n   276\tWe can now prove the soundness theorem for the Pauli basis test, stated as Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-test}.\n   277\t\n   278\t\\begin{proof}\n  1410\t\n  1411\t\t\n  1412\t\t\n  1413\t\\end{proof}\n  1414\t\n  1415\t \\subsection{Pulling the $X$ and $Z$ measurements apart}\n  1416\t\\label{sec:separating}\n  1417\t\n  1418\t\n  1419\tRecall the decoding map of the low-degree code defined in \\Cref{sec:ld-encoding}. This map takes in a polynomial $g \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ and returns a string $\\coded(g) \\in \\F_q^{\\nqubits}$ consisting of the evaluations of $g$ on the points in the hypercube $\\{0,1\\}^m$.\n  1420\tWe now use this decoding map to construct a new set of measurement operators out of $\\hat{S}$.\n  1421\tFirst, we introduce some notation. For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and all degree $d$ polynomials $g: \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$ define projectors\n  1422\t\\[ \\hat{S}^X_g = \\sum_{g_\\zpt} \\hat{S}_{g,g_\\zpt} \\quad  \\text{and}  \\quad \\hat{S}^Z_g = \\sum_{g_\\xpt} \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g}\\;,\\]\n  1423\twhere the measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\}$  is given by \\Cref{lem:qld-4-7}.\n  1424\t\n  1425\tFor all $W \\in \\{X, Z\\}$, $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^{\\nqubits}$, and $a \\in \\F_q$, define the projector\n  1426\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-tauwu}\n  1427\t \\tau^{W}_{a}(\\tilde{u}) = \\sum_{h \\in \\F_q^{\\nqubits} \\,:\\, h \\cdot \\tilde{u} = a} \\tau^W_{h}\n  1428\t\\end{equation}\n  1429\twhere recall that $\\tau^W_h$ is the projector corresponding to measuring $(\\C^q)^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$ in the $W$ basis and obtaining $h \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ as the outcome. For all $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ define the projective measurement $\\{ \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ by \n  1430\t\t\\begin{equation}\n  1431\t\t\\label{eq:tilde_M}\n  1432\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a =  \\sum_{g} \\hat{S}^W_g \\otimes \\tau_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}^W(\\tilde{u})\\;.\n  1433\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1434\t\tIf $\\hat{S}^W_g$ is viewed as an operator acting on registers $\\alice \\alice'$ (resp. $\\bob \\bob'$) then we view $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$ as an operator acting on registers $\\alice \\alice' \\alice''$ (resp. $\\bob \\bob' \\bob''$). (When necessary we will indicate via subscripts which registers the operators are acting on.)\n  1435\tThe $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$ are projective because the $\\hat{S}^W_g$ and $\\tau^W$ operators are projective.\n  1436\t\n  1437\t\n  1438\t\tFor all $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$ define the observable\n  1439\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-tildewj}\n  1440\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) = \\sum_a (-1)^{\\tr(e_j a)} \\, \\tilde{M}_a^{W,\\tilde{u}} \\;,\n  1441\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1442\t\twhere $\\{e_j\\}_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}}$ denotes the self-dual basis for $\\F_q$ over $\\F_2$ specified in \\Cref{sec:subfields}. Let $\\tau^W$ be the generalized Pauli observable defined in Section~\\ref{sec:generalized-pauli}. Observe that\n  1443\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1444\t\t\\Big ( \\sum_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\coded(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\Big ) \\otimes \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})\n  1445\t\t&= \\sum_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\sum_h (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\coded(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j(\\tilde{u} \\cdot h))} \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\otimes \\tau^W_h\\\\\n  1446\t\t&= \\sum_{g_W} \\sum_a  (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\tilde{u} \\cdot\\coded(g_W) + a))} \\hat{S}_{g_W}^W \\otimes \\Big( \\sum_{h:\\, h\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}\\tau^W_h\\Big)\\\\\n  1447\t\t\t&= \\sum_{g_W} \\sum_a  (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\tilde{u} \\cdot\\coded(g_W) + a))} \\hat{S}_{g_W}^W \\otimes \\tau^W_a(\\tilde{u})\\\\\n  1448\t\t\t&= \t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\;,\n  1449\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1450\t\twhere the first equality uses the definition of $\\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$, the second uses the definition of $\\hat{S}^W_{g_W}$ and regroups terms, the third uses the definition~\\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} of $\\tau^W_a(\\tilde{u})$, and the last is by a change of variables. These equations in particular show that the observable $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ is Hermitian and squares to the identity, because the measurement $\\{ \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\}$ is projective. \n  1451\t\tFurthermore, it can be verified by direct calculation that the observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ satisfy the same relations as the generalized Pauli group. Specifically, for all $j,j' \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$,\n  1452\t\t\\[\n  1453\t\t\t\\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v}) = \\begin{cases}\n  1454\t\t\t\\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v}) \\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u})  & \\qquad j \\neq j' \\\\\n  1455\t\t\t(-1)^{\\tr((e_j \\tilde{u}) \\cdot (e_j \\tilde{v}))} \\tilde{Z}^{j}(\\tilde{v}) \\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u}) & \\qquad j = j'\n  1456\t\t\t\\end{cases}~.\n  1457\t\t\\]\n  1458\t\tThe next lemma shows that the measurements $\\{ \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\}$  and observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$  are consistent with the original points measurements, and are self-consistent. \n  1459\t\t\n  1460\t\n  1461\t\n  1462\t\n  1463\t\\begin{lemma}\n  1464\t  \\label{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}\n  1465\tLet $\\delta_S$ as in Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}.\n  1466\tFor all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, all $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ and $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, the measurements $\\{ \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ and observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ satisfy the following properties:\n  1467\t\\begin{enumerate}\n  1468\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, for all $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, and on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1469\t\t\\[\n  1470\t\t\t\\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\simeq_{\\delta_S} M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}\n  1471\t\t\\]\n  1472\t\tand\n  1473\t\t\\[\n  1474\t\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\simeq_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\;.\n  1475\t\t\\]\t\n  1476\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) On average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$,\n  1477\t\t\\[\n  1478\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})\\,.\n  1479\t\t\\]\n  1480\t\\end{enumerate}\n  1481\t\\end{lemma}\n  1482\t\n  1483\t\\begin{proof}\n  1484\tWe show the first item of the lemma for the case when $M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a$ acts on register $\\alice$ and $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a$ acts on registers $\\bob \\bob' \\bob''$ (the argument for when the $\\alice$ and $\\bob$ registers are interchanged proceeds analogously):\n  1485\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1486\t\t\t&\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\otimes \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1487\t\t\t&= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_{g,a} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a)_{\\alice} \\otimes (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_{\\coded(g) \\cdot \\ind_m(u) -a}(\\ind_m(u)))_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1488\t\t\t&= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{\\coded(g) \\cdot \\ind_m(u)})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\otimes (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1489\t\t\t&= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\otimes (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1490\t\t\t&\\geq 1 - \\delta_S\\;.\n  1491\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1492\t\tThe second line follows from expanding the definition of $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a$ using~\\eqref{eq:tilde_M}, the third line follows from the definition of $\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_a$ given in Section~\\ref{sec:expanding}, the fourth line follows from the fact that $\\coded(g) \\cdot \\ind_m(u) = g(u)$, and the fifth line follows from \\Cref{lem:qld-4-7}. This implies the first item of the Lemma.\n  1493\t\n  1494\t\n  1495\tTo show the second item we first argue that, on average over $\\tilde{u}$, \n  1496\t\t\\begin{equation}\n  1497\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-pulling-cons}\n  1498\t\t\t\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}.\n  1499\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1500\t\tThis follows because on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$ and $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ we have\n  1501\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1502\t\t\t&\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-0} \\\\\n  1503\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_S}& \\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice'\\alice''} \\cdot \\Big ( \\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes  (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-1} \\\\\n  1504\t\t\t=_{\\phantom{\\delta_S}}& \\sum_g \\Big ( (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}(\\tilde{u}))_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big(\\sum_{r,s: r + s = g(u)} (M^{(\\Point,W),u}_r)_{\\bob} \\otimes (\\tau_s^W(\\ind_m(u)))_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-2} \\\\\n  1505\t\t\t=_{\\phantom{\\delta_S}}& \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}}  (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''} \\otimes (M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)})_{\\bob}~. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-2b}\n  1506\t\t\\end{align}\n  1507\t\tThe first inequality follows from right-multiplying~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-0} by the approximation~\\eqref{eq:qld-sg-cons} in \\Cref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (to be proved below) and using Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}. Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-2} follows from expanding the definition of $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$.  To obtain Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-2b}, we expanded the definitions of $\\tau^W_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}(\\tilde{u})$ and $\\tau^W_s(\\ind_m(u))$ using~\\cref{eq:def-tauwu}, and the fact that $h \\cdot \\ind_m(u) = g_h(u)$ (due to \\cref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}). Thus for all $g \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ and $s \\in \\F_q$ we have\n  1508\t\t\\[\n  1509\t\t\t\\tau^W_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}(\\tilde{u}) \\cdot \\tau^W_s(\\ind_m(u)) = \\sum_{\\substack{h: \\\\ (\\coded(g) - h) \\cdot \\tilde{u} = a \\\\ g_h(u) = s}} \\tau^W_h~.\n  1510\t\t\\]\n  1511\t\n  1512\t\n  1513\t\tUsing the self-consistency of $\\{M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\}$ shown in Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}, we then obtain\n  1514\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1515\t\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-2b}} &\\approx_{\\eps} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\cdot \\Big((M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)})_{\\alice} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big ( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\Big)_{\\bob} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-3} \\\\\n  1516\t\t\t&\\approx_{0}\\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\cdot \\Big((M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)})_{\\alice} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big ( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\Big)_{\\bob} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-3a} \\\\\n  1517\t\t\t&= \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\cdot \\Big((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big ( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\Big)_{\\bob} \\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-3b}\n  1518\t\t\\end{align}\n  1519\t\tThe ``$\\approx_0$'' in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3a} indicates that the left hand side is equal to the right hand side when applied to the state $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$. Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3a} follows from the self-consistency of the operators $\\{\\tau^W_h\\}$, and Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3b} follows from the definition of $\\{ \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\}$. Using the fact that $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} = \\sum_{h'} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$, we get that \n  1520\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1521\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3b}} & \\approx_0 \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\otimes \\sum_{h'} \\tau_{h'}^W \\otimes \\tau_{h'}^W\\Big)_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-4} \\\\\n  1522\t\t\t&= \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-5}\n  1523\t\\end{align}\n  1524\t\tLine~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-5} follows from~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-4} by expanding the definition of $\\{ \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\}$. Next, we argue that, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1525\t\\begin{align}\n  1526\t\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-5}} &\\approx_{\\delta_S} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\otimes \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big)~. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-7}\n  1527\t\t\t\\end{align}\n  1528\t\t\tWe show this by bounding the magnitude of the difference. Using the fact that $\\hat{S}^W_g$ and $\\tau^W_h$ are projective, we have:\n  1529\t\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1530\t\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\|\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\otimes (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\, \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\notag \\\\\n  1531\t      =& \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h'}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big(((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\notag \\\\\n  1532\t      & \\hspace{20em} \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})^2_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\notag \\\\\n  1533\t\t\t\\leq& \\E_u \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\label{eq:qld-pulling-8} \\\\\n  1534\t\t\t\\leq& \\delta_S\\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-9}\n  1535\t\t\t\\end{align}\n  1536\t\t\tThe inequality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-8} follows from the fact that for all $g,h$, the sum $\\sum_{h'} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})^2_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''}$ is at most $\\Id$. The inequality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-9} follows from the second item of \\Cref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (to be proved below). \n  1537\t\t\tNext, we show that on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1538\t\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1539\t\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7}} &=\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-9a} \\\\\n  1540\t\t\t&\\approx_{O(\\delta_S + \\sqrt{\\eps})} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-10} \\\\\n  1541\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_S} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-11} \\\\\n  1542\t\t\t&\\approx_{md/q} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g -g_h \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big)~. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-12}\n  1543\t\t\t\\end{align}\n  1544\t\t\tBefore we justify the sequence of steps to go from Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7} to Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}, we first argue how this establishes Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons}, and hence the second item of the Lemma statement.\n  1545\t\t\t\n  1546\t\t\tAbsorbing the error terms $O(md/q)$ and $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ into $\\delta_S$, we have shown that \n  1547\t\t\t\\[\n  1548\t\t\t\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g -g_h \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big).\n  1549\t\t\t\\]\n  1550\t\t\tNote that the conditions $(\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a$ and $g - g_h = g' - g_{h'}$ are equivalent to the conditions $(\\coded(g') - h') \\cdot \\tilde{u} = a$ and $g - g_h = g' - g_{h'}$. Thus the expression in~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12} is symmetric between $g,h$ and $g',h'$, and so an analogous derivation shows that $\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}$ is $\\delta_S$-close to~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}, and thus $\\delta_S$-close to $\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''}$. This shows~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons}. Since $\\{ \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\}$ is projective, by using Item 2 of \\Cref{fact:agreement}, followed by \\Cref{fact:data-processing}, and then followed by Item 1 of \\Cref{fact:agreement}, we get for all $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, on average over $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$,\n  1551\t\\[\n  1552\t\t(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}\n  1553\t\\]\n  1554\twhere the answer summation is over $b \\in \\F_2$. Finally, the second item of the Lemma follows from applying~\\Cref{lem:povm-to-obs}.\n  1555\t\n  1556\t\t\tWe now return to justifying the sequence of steps between Lines~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7} and~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}. The equality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-9a} follows by left-multiplying~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7} by $I_{\\bob \\bob'} = \\sum_{g'} (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'}$. \n  1557\t\t\t\n  1558\t\t\t\\paragraph{The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-10}.} The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-10} follows by bounding the magnitude of the difference:\t\n  1559\t\\begin{align}\n  1560\t\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\| \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) \\neq (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\notag \\\\\n  1561\t&= \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) \\neq (g -g_h)(u)}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big(\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\notag\\\\\n  1562\t      &\\hspace{20em}\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\notag \\\\\n  1563\t\t\t&\\leq \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h',a': \\\\ a' \\neq g'(u)}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big(\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a'} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\notag \\\\\n  1564\t\t\t&= \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-13a} \\\\\n  1565\t\t\t&\\approx_{O(\\sqrt{\\eps})} \\E_u \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\otimes ( \\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\label{eq:qld-pulling-13} \\\\\n  1566\t\t\t&\\leq \\delta_S \\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-14}\n  1567\t\\end{align}\n  1568\tThe approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13} follows from the following calculation:\n  1569\t\\begin{align}\n  1570\t\t&\\Big | \\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13}} - \\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13a}} \\Big | \\notag \\\\\n  1571\t\t&\\leq \\Big | \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\cdot ((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big | \\label{eq:qld-pulling-15} \\\\\n  1572\t\t& \\qquad + \\Big | \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'}) \\cdot (\\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\cdot (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big | \\label{eq:qld-pulling-15a}\n  1573\t\\end{align}\n  1574\tUsing Cauchy-Schwarz, we can bound the first term on the right hand side of the inequality by\n  1575\t\\begin{align}\n  1576\t\t&\\Biggl( \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\Biggr)^{1/2} \\cdot \\notag \\\\\n  1577\t\t&\\Biggl( \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'}) \\cdot (\\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\cdot \\notag\\\\\n  1578\t  & \\hspace{12em}((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'})  \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\Biggr)^{1/2} \\notag \\\\\n  1579\t\t\\leq & \\sqrt{1} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\eps} \\notag\n  1580\t\\end{align}\n  1581\tThe inequality follows from the self-consistency of $\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a}$, as established in \\Cref{lem:qld-comm-cons}. Similarly, we can bound the second term in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-15a} by $\\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n  1582\tThis establishes the approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13}. \n  1583\t\n  1584\tThe inequality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-14} follows from \\Cref{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob} of \\Cref{lem:qld-4-7}. This concludes the derivation of the approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-10}.\n  1585\t\n  1586\t\\paragraph{The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-11}.} We establish this approximation by bounding the magnitude of the difference:\n  1587\t\\begin{align*}\n  1588\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\| \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}))_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\\\\n  1589\t\t=& \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) \\\\ \\quad = (g -g_h)(u)}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\otimes ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\notag \\\\\n  1590\t  &\\hspace{20em}(\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}))_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\\\\n  1591\t\t\\leq& \\E_u \\sum_{g'} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}))_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1592\t\t\\leq& \\delta_S \\;.\n  1593\t\\end{align*}\n  1594\tThe last inequality follows from the second item of \\Cref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (to be proved below). \n  1595\t\n  1596\t\\paragraph{The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}.} We establish this approximation by bounding the magnitude of the difference:\n  1597\t\\begin{align*}\n  1598\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\| \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} \\neq g -g_h \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\\\\n  1599\t\t&= \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} \\neq g -g_h}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\cdot \\E_u \\mathbf{1}[(g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u)] \\\\\n  1600\t\t&\\leq md/q.\n  1601\t\\end{align*}\n  1602\tThe last inequality follows from the Schwartz-Zippel lemma: two distinct polynomials of total degree at most $md$ cannot agree on more than $md/q$ fraction of points. \n  1603\t\n  1604\t\t\t\n  1605\t\n  1606\t\\end{proof}\n  1607\t\n  1608\t\n  1609\tWe now give a proof of the following Lemma, which was used in several derivations in \\Cref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}. \n  1610\t\\begin{lemma}\n  1611\t\\label{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper}\n  1612\t\tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$, we have that\n  1613\t\t\\begin{gather}\n  1614\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-sg-cons}\n  1615\t\t\t\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id\n  1616\t\t\\end{gather}\n  1617\t\tand\n  1618\t\t\\begin{gather}\n  1619\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-sg-cons2}\n  1620\t\t\t(\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} 0\n  1621\t\t\\end{gather}\n  1622\t\twhere the answer summation is over $g \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$.\n  1623\t      Furthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations\n  1624\t      also hold.\n  1625\t\\end{lemma}\n  1626\t\\begin{proof}\n  1627\tWe first prove \\Cref{eq:qld-sg-cons}. From \\Cref{eq:qld-s-point-con-alice} of \\Cref{lem:qld-4-7} we have that on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1628\t\\begin{equation}\n  1629\t\\label{eq:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper0}\n  1630\t\t  \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{g(u)})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S\n  1631\t\\end{equation}\n  1632\twhich is equivalent to $\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{g(u)})_{\\bob \\alice''}  \\simeq_{\\delta_S} \\Id$. By \\Cref{fact:agreement}, we have that\n  1633\t\\[\n  1634\t\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{g(u)})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id.\n  1635\t\\]\n  1636\t\n  1637\tWe now prove \\Cref{eq:qld-sg-cons2}. \\Cref{eq:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper0} and \\Cref{fact:agreement} shows that\n  1638\t\\[ (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\bob \\alice''}\\]\n  1639\twhere the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$. Left-multiplying this expression by $(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\alice \\alice'}$ and using \\Cref{fact:add-a-proj} we get\n  1640\t\\begin{align*}\n  1641\t(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\alice \\alice'} &\\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\\\\n  1642\t&\\approx_\\eps (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\alice \\alice'}\n  1643\t\\end{align*}\n  1644\twhere the second approximation follows from the following calculation:\n  1645\t\\begin{align}\n  1646\t\t& \\E_u \\sum_a \\left \\| (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\cdot \\Big ( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\alice \\alice'} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\Big ) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\right \\|^2  \\\\\n  1647\t\t\\leq & \\E_u \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big ( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\alice \\alice'} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\Big )^2 \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1648\t\t= & \\E_u \\sum_a \\left \\| (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\alice \\alice'} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\right \\|^2 \\\\\n  1649\t\t\\leq & \\eps\n  1650\t\\end{align}\n  1651\tThe first inequality follows from the fact that $\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]} \\leq \\Id$ for all $a$, and the second inequality follows from the self-consistency of $\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a}$ (established in \\Cref{lem:qld-comm-cons}). Thus\n  1652\t\\[\n  1653\t\t(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\approx_{O(\\delta_S + \\eps)} (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\alice \\alice'}~.\n  1654\t\\]\n  1655\tOn the other hand, notice that\n  1656\t\\begin{align*}\n  1657\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_g \\left \\| (\\hat{S}^W_{g} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\right \\|^2 \\\\\n  1658\t\t=& \\E_u \\sum_g \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{g(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1659\t\t=& \\E_u \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1660\t\t\\leq& O(\\delta_S + \\eps).\n  1661\t\\end{align*}\n  1662\t\\Cref{eq:qld-sg-cons2} is thus obtained by absorbing $\\eps$ into the function $\\delta_S$.  \n  1663\t\\end{proof}\n  1664\t\t\n  1665\t\n  1666\t The next lemma shows that we can construct local unitaries $V_A,V_B$ from the measurements $\\hat{S}$  such that the shared state $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ is close to $\\ket{\\aux} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$ for some state $\\ket{\\aux}$, and the observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ are mapped to Pauli observables $\\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$ acting on $\\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$. Using the consistency between the $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ measurements and the points measurements $\\{M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ established in \\Cref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}, and the consistency between the points measurements and the ``total Pauli measurements'' $\\{M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h\\}_{h \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits}$ established in \\Cref{lem:qld-win-implications}, we deduce that the total Pauli measurements must be close (up to the local unitaries $V_A,V_B$) to the Pauli projectors $\\{\\tau^W_h\\}_{h \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits}$ acting on $\\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$.\n  1667\t\n  1668\t\n  1669\t\\begin{lemma}\n  1670\t\\label{lem:qld-unitary}\n  1671\tThere exists a function $\\delta_\\qld(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ (where $\\delta_S$ is defined in \\Cref{lem:qld-4-7}) and unitaries $V_\\alice$ acting on registers $\\alice \\alice' \\alice''$ and $V_\\bob$ acting on $\\bob \\bob' \\bob''$ such that \n  1672\t\\begin{enumerate}\n  1673\t\t\\item There exists a state $\\ket{\\aux}$ on registers $\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'$ such that\n  1674\t\t\\[\n  1675\t\t\t\\left \\| V_\\alice \\otimes V_\\bob \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice'' \\bob \\bob' \\bob''} - \\ket{\\aux}_{\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}_{\\alice'' \\bob''} \\right \\|^2 \\leq \\delta_\\qld.\n  1676\t\t\\]\n  1677\t\t\n  1678\t\t\\item For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}, h \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$\n  1679\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1680\t\t\tV_\\alice \\, \\Big(M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\Big)_{\\alice} \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_\\qld} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''} \\\\\n  1681\t\t\t V_\\bob \\, \\Big(M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h  \\Big)_{\\bob}\\, V_\\bob^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_\\qld} \\Id_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_h)_{\\bob''}\n  1682\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1683\t\twhere the $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\aux}_{\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}_{\\alice'' \\bob''}$. \n  1684\t\\end{enumerate}\n  1685\t\\end{lemma}\n  1686\t\n  1687\t\\begin{proof}\n  1688\t\n  1689\tDefine the linear map\n  1690\t\\[\n  1691\t\tV_\\alice = \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)))_{\\alice ''}\\;,\n  1692\t\\]\n  1693\twhere the $\\tau^X(\\cdot),\\tau^Z(\\cdot)$ act on $(\\C^q)^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$. (Also note that the argument of $\\tau^X$ is $\\coded(g_Z)$ and the argument of $\\tau^Z$ is $\\coded(g_X)$.) We verify the unitarity of $V_\\alice$:\n  1694\t\\begin{align*}\n  1695\t\tV_\\alice V_\\alice^\\dagger &= \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)) \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) )_{\\alice ''} \\\\\n  1696\t\t&= \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes \\Id_{\\alice ''} \\\\\n  1697\t\t&= \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''}\n  1698\t\\end{align*}\n  1699\twhere in the first line we used that the measurement $\\{ \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\}$  is projective and in the second line we used that the $\\tau^Z$ and $\\tau^X$ observables are self-inverse. The unitary $V_\\bob$ is defined analogously.\n  1700\t\n  1701\tLet $\\{e_j\\}_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}}$ denote the self-dual basis for $\\F_q$ over $\\F_2$ specified in \\Cref{sec:subfields}, and let $\\tilde{W}^j$ be the observables introduced in~\\eqref{eq:def-tildewj}. Conjugating the observable $\\tilde{W}^j$ acting on the registers $\\alice \\alice' \\alice''$ by the unitary $V_\\alice$ we get\n  1702\t\\begin{align*}\n  1703\t\t&V_\\alice \\, \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger \\\\\n  1704\t  =& \\sum_{g_X, g_Z} (-1)^{\\tr( e_j(\\coded(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\Big( \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}  \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes \\Big ( \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)) \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\\\\n  1705\t  &\\hspace{24em}\\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X))^\\dagger \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z))^\\dagger \\Big)_{\\alice ''} \\\\\n  1706\t\t =& \\sum_{g_X, g_Z} \\Big( \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}  \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes \\Big ( \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\Big)_{\\alice ''} \\\\\n  1707\t\t =& \\Id_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes \\Big( \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\Big)_{\\alice ''}\\;,\n  1708\t\\end{align*}\n  1709\twhere in the third line we used the (anti-)commutation relation\n  1710\t\\[\n  1711\t\t\\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)) \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = (-1)^{\\tr( e_j(\\coded(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)) \\;.\n  1712\t\\]\n  1713\tAn entirely analogous calculation shows that $V_\\bob \\, \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\, V_\\bob^\\dagger = \\Id_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes \\Big( \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\Big)_{\\bob ''}$.\n  1714\t\n  1715\tNext, from the self-consistency property of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j$ specified in \\Cref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} we get that for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n  1716\t\\[\n  1717\t\t\\E_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}} \\E_{\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\left ( \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\otimes \\Id - \\Id \\otimes \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\right)^\\dagger \\left ( \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\otimes \\Id - \\Id \\otimes \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\right) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\leq \\delta_S\\;,\n  1718\t\\]\n  1719\twhich, since $\\tilde{W}^j$ is Hermitian, is equivalent to\n  1720\t\\begin{equation}\n  1721\t\\label{eq:qld-unitary-1}\n  1722\t\\E_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}} \\E_{\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\otimes \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2\\;.\n  1723\t\\end{equation}\n  1724\tLet $\\ket{\\vartheta} = V_\\alice \\otimes V_\\bob \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$, and for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ define the operator\n  1725\t\\begin{align*}\n  1726\t\tH_W &= \\E_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}} \\E_{\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits} \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\otimes \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\\\\n  1727\t\t\t&= \\E_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}} \\Big ( \\E_{s \\in \\F_q} \\tau^W(e_j s) \\otimes \\tau^W(e_j s) \\Big)^{\\otimes \\nqubits} \\\\\n  1728\t\t\t&= \\Big ( \\E_{s \\in \\F_q} \\tau^W(s) \\otimes \\tau^W(s) \\Big)^{\\otimes \\nqubits}\n  1729\t\\end{align*}\n  1730\twhere in the second line we used that for all $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$, $\\tau^W(\\tilde{u}) = \\bigotimes_{i = 1}^\\nqubits \\tau^W(\\tilde{u}_i)$ with $\\tau^W(\\tilde{u}_i)$ being the generalized Pauli $W$ observable acting on the $i$-th qudit. In the third line, we used the fact that for every fixed $j$, we have $\\E_{s \\in \\F_q} \\tau^W(e_j s) \\otimes \\tau^W(e_j s) = \\E_{s \\in \\F_q} \\tau^W(s) \\otimes \\tau^W(s)$. \\Cref{eq:qld-unitary-1} is then equivalent to \n  1731\t\\[\n  1732\t\t\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2\\;,\n  1733\t\\]\n  1734\tfor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$. \n  1735\t\n  1736\tObserve that\n  1737\t\\begin{align*}\n  1738\t\t\\E_{s,s'} \\tau^X(s) \\tau^Z(s') \\otimes \\tau^X(s) \\tau^Z(s') &= \\E_{s,s'} \\sum_{a,b \\in \\F_q} (-1)^{\\tr(as')} \\ketbra{a+s}{a} \\otimes (-1)^{\\tr(bs')} \\ketbra{b+s}{b} \\\\\n  1739\t\t&= \\E_{s} \\sum_{a\\in \\F_q} \\ketbra{a+s}{a} \\otimes \\ketbra{a+s}{a} \\\\\n  1740\t\t&= \\proj{\\EPR_q}\n  1741\t\\end{align*}\n  1742\twhere to go from the first to the second line we used \\Cref{lem:cancellation}. This implies that $H_X H_Z = \\Big ( \\E_{s,s' \\in \\F_q} \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') \\otimes \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') \\Big)^{\\otimes \\nqubits} = \\ketbra{\\EPR_q}{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$.\n  1743\tBy the triangle inequality, we have\n  1744\t\\[\n  1745\t\t\\left \\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\right\\| \\leq \\left \\| \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} \\right\\| + \\left \\| \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\right\\|\\;.\n  1746\t\\]\n  1747\tNote that for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n  1748\t\\[\n  1749\t\t\\left \\| \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} \\right\\|^2 = 1 - 2\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} + \\bra{\\vartheta} H_W^2 \\ket{\\vartheta} \\leq 2 - 2\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} \\leq \\delta_S\\;,\n  1750\t\\]\n  1751\twhere in the inequality we used that $H_W \\leq \\Id$. Furthermore,\n  1752\t\\begin{align*}\n  1753\t\t\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W^2 \\ket{\\vartheta} &=\\| H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2\\\\\n  1754\t\t &= \\| \\ket{\\vartheta} + (H_W - \\Id) \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\\\\n  1755\t\t &\\geq (\\|\\ket{\\vartheta}\\| - \\| (H_W - \\Id) \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|)^2 \\\\\n  1756\t\t &\\geq (1 - \\delta_S)^2 \\\\\n  1757\t\t &\\geq 1 - 2\\delta_S\\;. \n  1758\t\\end{align*}\n  1759\tTherefore \n  1760\t\\begin{align*}\n  1761\t2\\sqrt{\\delta_S} &\\geq \\left \\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\right\\|^2  \\\\\n  1762\t&= \\bra{\\vartheta} H_X^2 \\ket{\\vartheta} + \\bra{\\vartheta} H_Z^2 \\ket{\\vartheta} - 2\\bra{\\vartheta} H_X H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\\\\n  1763\t&\\geq 2 - 4\\delta_S - 2\\bra{\\vartheta} H_X H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\;,\n  1764\t\\end{align*}\n  1765\twhich implies that \n  1766\t\\[\n  1767\t\t\\bra{\\vartheta} (\\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\proj{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits})_{\\alice'' \\bob''}) \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - 2\\delta_S - \\sqrt{\\delta_S} \\;.\n  1768\t\\]\n  1769\tDefine the unnormalized state $\\ket{\\aux_0}$ on the registers $\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'$ as\n  1770\t\\[\n  1771\t\t\\ket{\\aux_0}_{\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'} = (\\bra{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits})_{\\alice'' \\bob''} \\cdot \\ket{\\vartheta}_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice'' \\bob \\bob' \\bob''}\n  1772\t\\]\n  1773\tand define the normalized state $\\ket{\\aux} = \\frac{1}{\\| \\, \\ket{\\aux_0} \\, \\|} \\ket{\\aux_0}$. \n  1774\tThe inner product between $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ and $\\ket{\\aux} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$ can thus be evaluated as\n  1775\t\\begin{align*}\n  1776\t\t\\bra{\\vartheta} (\\ket{\\aux} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}) &= \\frac{1}{\\| \\, \\ket{\\aux_0} \\, \\|} \\cdot \\bra{\\vartheta} (\\ket{\\aux_0} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}) \\\\\n  1777\t\t&= \\frac{1}{\\| \\, \\ket{\\aux_0} \\, \\|} \\cdot \\bra{\\vartheta} (\\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\proj{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits})_{\\alice'' \\bob''}) \\ket{\\vartheta} \\\\\n  1778\t\t&= \\frac{1}{\\| \\, \\ket{\\aux_0} \\, \\|} \\cdot \\| \\, \\ket{\\aux_0} \\, \\|^2 \\\\\n  1779\t\t&= \\| \\, \\ket{\\aux_0} \\, \\| \\\\\n  1780\t\t&\\geq \\sqrt{1 - 2\\delta_S - \\sqrt{\\delta_S}} \\\\\n  1781\t\t&\\geq 1 - O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})\\;.\n  1782\t\\end{align*}\n  1783\tThis implies $\\| \\ket{\\vartheta} - \\ket{\\aux} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits} \\|^2 \\leq O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$, which establishes the first item of the lemma.\n  1784\t\n  1785\tWe now establish the second item of the lemma. In what follows, all $\\simeq$ and $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$. From \\Cref{lem:qld-win-implications} we have the following implications: for all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1786\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1787\t\t\\label{eq:qld-unitary-2}\n  1788\t\tM^{(\\Pauli,W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} &\\simeq_\\eps \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\;, \\\\\n  1789\t\t\\label{eq:qld-unitary-3}\n  1790\t\t\\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a &\\simeq_\\eps  M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}\\;,\n  1791\t\t\\end{align}\n  1792\t\twhere $M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} = \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = a} M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h$. \n  1793\tFrom \\Cref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} we get that\n  1794\t\t\\begin{equation}\n  1795\t\t\\label{eq:qld-unitary-4}\n  1796\t\t\tM^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\simeq_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''}  \\otimes \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a\\;.\n  1797\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1798\tUsing \\Cref{fact:triangle-for-simeq} with Equations \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-2}, \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-3}, and \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-4}, we get \n  1799\t\t\\begin{equation}\n  1800\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-unitary-5}\n  1801\t\t\tM^{(\\Pauli,W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''}  \\otimes \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a\\;,\n  1802\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1803\t\twhere we used that $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$. \n  1804\t\t\n  1805\t\tNext, we have for all $u \\in \\F_q^m$ \n  1806\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1807\t\t\t&V_\\bob \\, \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\, V_\\bob^\\dagger \\notag \\\\\n  1808\t\t\t= &\\sum_{g_X, g_Z} \\Big( \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}  \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes \\Big ( \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)) \\tau^W_{g_W(u) - a}(\\ind_m(u))    \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X))^\\dagger \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z))^\\dagger \\Big)_{\\bob ''} \\notag \\\\\n  1809\t\t\t= &\\sum_{g_X, g_Z} \\Big( \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}  \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = g_W(u) - a} \\Big ( \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z)) \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X)) \\tau^W_h   \\tau^Z(\\coded(g_X))^\\dagger \\tau^X(\\coded(g_Z))^\\dagger \\Big)_{\\bob ''} \\notag \\\\\n  1810\t\t\t= &\\sum_{g_X, g_Z} \\Big( \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}  \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = g_W(u) - a} \\Big ( \\tau^W_{h + \\coded(g_W)}   \\Big)_{\\bob ''} \\notag \\\\\n  1811\t\t= &\\sum_{g_X, g_Z} \\Big( \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}  \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = a} \\Big ( \\tau^W_{h}   \\Big)_{\\bob ''} \\notag \\\\\n  1812\t= &\\Id_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_{[g_h(u)=a]})_{\\bob''}\\;,\t\t\\label{eq:qld-unitary-6}\n  1813\t\t\\end{align}\n  1814\t\twhere in the second line we expanded out the definitions of $V_\\bob$ and $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a$ (defined in \\cref{eq:tilde_M}); in the third line we expanded out the definition of $\\tau^W_{g_W(u) - a}(\\ind_m(u))$ (defined in \\cref{eq:def-tauwu}) and used the fact that for all $h \\in \\F_q^M$, the inner product $h \\cdot \\ind_m(u)$ is equal to $g_h(u)$; in the fourth line we used the identities\n  1815\t\t\\[\n  1816\t\t\t\\tau^X(s) \\tau^Z_h \\tau^X(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^Z_{h + s} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\tau^Z(s) \\tau^X_h \\tau^Z(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^X_{h + s}~;\n  1817\t\t\\]\n  1818\t\tand in the fifth line is due to the following short calculation: for all fixed $g_W$ we have\n  1819\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1820\t\t\t\\sum_{h : g_h(u) = g_W(u) - a}  \\tau^W_{h + \\coded(g_W)} = \\sum_{h: g_W(u) - g_h(u) = a} \\tau^W_{h + \\coded(g_W)} = \\sum_{h: g_W(u) + g_h(u) = a} \\tau^W_{h + \\coded(g_W)} = \\sum_{h': g_{h'}(u) = a} \\tau^W_{h'}~.\n  1821\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1822\t\tThe second equality from the fact that $\\F_q$ is a field of characteristic $2$ (so subtraction is the same as addition), and the third equality follows from the fact that letting $h' = h + \\coded(g_W)$, we have $g_{h'} = g_h + g_W$ (in other words, the low-degree encoding $h \\mapsto g_h$ is linear in $h$). \n  1823\t\t\n  1824\t\t\n  1825\t\n  1826\t\n  1827\tLet $\\ket{\\Delta} = \\ket{\\aux} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$. We show that $V_\\alice \\, \\Big(M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\Big)_{\\alice} \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger \\approx_{\\delta_\\qld} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''}$ (the argument for the operators acting on registers $\\bob \\bob' \\bob''$ proceeds identically). \n  1828\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1829\t\t\t&\\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} \\left ( V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger - (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''}  \\right)^\\dagger \\left ( V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger - (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''} \\right) \\ket{\\Delta} \\notag \\\\\n  1830\t\t\t&= 2 - 2 \\Re \\, \\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice\\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger  \\, (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''} \\ket{\\Delta} \\notag \\\\\n  1831\t\t\t&= 2 - 2 \\, \\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger  \\, \\otimes (\\tau^W_h)_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\Delta} \\label{eq:qld-unitary-7}\n  1832\t\\end{align}\n  1833\t\twhere in the last line we used that $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''} \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits} = (\\tau^W_h)_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$.\n  1834\t\tWe now claim that\n  1835\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1836\t\t&  \\, \\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger  \\, \\otimes (\\tau^W_h)_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\Delta}  \\notag \\\\\n  1837\t\t&\\approx_{md/q}  \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{h, h' : \\\\ g_h(u) = g_{h'}(u)}} \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger  \\, \\otimes (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\Delta} \\label{eq:qld-unitary-8} \\\\\n  1838\t\t&=  \\E_u \\sum_{a \\in \\F_q} \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger \\, \\otimes (\\tau^W_{[g_h(u)=a]})_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\Delta} \\notag \\\\\n  1839\t\t&=  \\E_u \\sum_{a \\in \\F_q} \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger \\, \\otimes V_\\bob  \\, \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\, V_\\bob^\\dagger \\ket{\\Delta} \\label{eq:qld-unitary-9}\n  1840\t\t\\end{align}\n  1841\t\twhere the equality in \\Cref{eq:qld-unitary-9} follows from the identity in \\Cref{eq:qld-unitary-6}, and the approximation in \\Cref{eq:qld-unitary-8} follows from the following calculation:\n  1842\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1843\t\t\t& \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{h \\neq h' : \\\\ g_h(u) = g_{h'}(u)}} \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger  \\, \\otimes (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\Delta}  \\\\\n  1844\t\t\t&= \\sum_{h \\neq h'} \\left( \\E_u \\mathbf{1}[g_h(u) = g_{h'}(u)] \\right) \\bra{\\Delta} V_\\alice \\, M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h \\, V_\\alice^\\dagger  \\, \\otimes (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\Delta} \\\\\n  1845\t\t\t&\\leq \\frac{md}{q}\\;.\n  1846\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1847\t\tIn the last line we used that for for distinct $h \\neq h'$, the polynomials $g_h \\neq g_{h'}$ and thus by the Schwartz-Zippel lemma can only agree on at most $md/q$ fraction of points $u \\in \\F_q^m$. \n  1848\t\t\n  1849\t\tContinuing from \\Cref{eq:qld-unitary-9}, we use the first item of the Lemma which shows that $\\| \\ket{\\Delta} - \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$ and equivalently $\\| V_\\alice^\\dagger \\otimes V_\\bob^\\dagger \\ket{\\Delta} - \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\|^2 \\leq O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$, so \n  1850\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1851\t\t\t\\text{\\cref{eq:qld-unitary-9}} &\\approx_{O(\\delta_S^{1/4})} \\E_u \\sum_{a \\in \\F_q} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\otimes \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1852\t\t\t&\\geq 1 - O(\\delta_S^{1/2})\\;,\n  1853\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1854\t        where the last line comes from \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-5}.\n  1855\t\tTogether with \\Cref{eq:qld-unitary-7}, \n  1856\t\t\\[\n  1857\t\t\\text{\\cref{eq:qld-unitary-7}} \\leq O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)\\;.\t\n  1858\t\t\\]\n  1859\t\tThis concludes the proof of the second item of the lemma.\n  1860\t\\end{proof}\n  1861\t\n  1862\tWe now prove \\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix}. \n  1863\t\\begin{proof}[Proof of \\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix}]\n  1864\t\tLet $\\strategy = (\\psi,M)$ be a projective strategy for $\\game^\\pauli_\\qldparams$ that succeeds with probability at least $1 - \\eps$, where $\\ket{\\psi}$ is a bipartite state on registers $\\alice \\bob$. As mentioned at the beginning of \\Cref{sec:commutation}, we assume that the state $\\ket{\\psi}$ is padded with sufficiently many ancilla $\\ket{0}$ qubits. Let $\\phi_\\alice$ be the following isometry mapping the register $\\alice$ to the registers $\\alice \\alice' \\alice''$ where $\\alice'$ and $\\alice''$ are isomorphic to $(\\C^q)^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$:\n  1865\t\t\\[\n  1866\t\t\t\\phi_\\alice: \\ket{\\theta}_\\alice \\mapsto V_\\alice (\\ket{\\theta}_\\alice \\otimes (\\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits})_{\\alice' \\alice''})\n  1867\t\t\\]\n  1868\t\twhere $V_\\alice$ is the unitary acting on $\\alice \\alice' \\alice''$ from \\Cref{lem:qld-unitary}. Define the isometry $\\phi_\\bob$ analogously. \\Cref{lem:qld-unitary} then implies that there exists a state $\\ket{\\aux}$ on registers $\\alice \\alice' \\bob \\bob'$ such that\n  1869\t\t\\[\n  1870\t\t\t\\left \\| \\phi_\\alice \\otimes \\phi_\\bob \\ket{\\psi} - \\ket{\\aux} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits} \\right \\|^2 \\leq \\delta_\\qld\n  1871\t\t\\]\n  1872\t\tand for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n  1873\t\t\\[\n  1874\t\t\t\\phi_\\alice \\, (M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h)_\\alice \\, \\phi_\\alice^\\dagger \\approx_{\\delta_\\qld} (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\phi_\\bob \\, (M^{(\\Pauli,W)}_h)_\\bob \\, \\phi_\\bob^\\dagger \\approx_{\\delta_\\qld} (\\tau^W_h)_{\\bob''}\n  1875\t\t\\]\n  1876\t\twhere the $\\approx$ statement holds with respect to the state $\\ket{\\aux} \\otimes \\ket{\\EPR_q}^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$. This shows that $\\game^\\pauli_\\qldparams$ is a self-test for the strategy $\\strategy^\\pauli$ with robustness $\\delta_\\qld(\\eps,m,d,q)$. We conclude the proof by making the identification of registers $\\alice \\alice'$ with $\\alice'$ and $\\bob \\bob'$ with $\\bob'$ to be consistent with the statement of \\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix}. We also note that, when unpacking the dependence on parameters $\\eps, m, d, q$, the function $\\delta_\\qld(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ has the form $a(md)^a(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for some universal constants $a > 1, 0 < b < 1$, as desired.\n  1877\t\\end{proof}\n  1878\t \n  1879\t\n  1880\t \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/0001/cf4cfa6eb26ad593c4e130d033b54e6a455a1ae1/diff.patch:5241:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_126","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '232,278p'\"","aggregated_output":"   232\t\\begin{proof}\n   233\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, lem:tildew-product-form, lem:twisted-commutation, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:indicator-vector, def:decoding-map, def:generalized-pauli}\n   234\t\tSince the measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ is projective (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}) and the $\\tau^X, \\tau^Z$ observables are self-inverse,\n   235\t\t\\[\n   236\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger = \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}''} = \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}\\;,\n   237\t\t\\]\n   238\t\tand the same computation gives $V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger V_{\\mathrm{A}} = \\Id$, so $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ is unitary; likewise $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$. For \\eqref{eq:v-swap-obs-conjugation}, conjugate the product form \\eqref{eq:tildewj-product-form} of $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:tildew-product-form}): by the orthogonality of the projectors $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$, only the diagonal terms survive, and the twisted commutation relation (Lemma~\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation})\n   239\t\t\\[\n   240\t\t\t\\tau^X(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)) \\, \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X)) \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = (-1)^{\\tr( e_j(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\, \\tau^X(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_Z)) \\, \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{Dec}(g_X))\n   241\t\t\\]\n   242\t\tcancels the sign $(-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))}$ term by term, leaving $\\sum_{g_X,g_Z} \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z} \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\Id \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$. For \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}, expand $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$ by \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu}; by the orthogonality of the $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$, conjugation replaces, in the term of $\\hat{S}^W_g$, each projector $\\tau^W_h$ by $\\tau^W_{h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)}$, using the identities $\\tau^X(s)\\, \\tau^Z_h \\,\\tau^X(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^Z_{h+s}$ and $\\tau^Z(s)\\, \\tau^X_h\\, \\tau^Z(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^X_{h+s}$. Substituting $h' = h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)$ turns the constraint $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = (\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)) - a$ into $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = -a = a$, since $\\F_q$ has characteristic $2$; the quantity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly, so no relation between it and $g(u)$ is used (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}). Since $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_{h'}(u)$ \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}, summing over $g$ gives \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}; the computation for $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ is identical.\n   243\t\\end{proof}\n   244\t\n   245\t\\begin{lemma}[Extraction of the EPR state and the Pauli observables]\\label{lem:qld-unitary}\n   246\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, def:v-swap-unitary, def:expanded-state, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:generalized-pauli, def:EPR, def:povm-distance}\n   247\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$, where $\\delta_S$ is the function of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, and there exist unitaries $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ acting on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ and $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$ acting on registers $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, such that the following hold.\n   248\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   249\t\t\t\\item There exists a state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$ such that\n   250\t\t\t\\[\n   251\t\t\t\t\\bigl\\| V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\bigr\\|^2 \\leq \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}\\;.\n   252\t\t\t\\]\n   253\t\t\t\\item For $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$,\n   254\t\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   255\t\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''}\\;, \\\\\n   256\t\t\t\tV_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   257\t\t\t\\end{align*}\n   258\t\t\twhere the $\\approx$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''}$, with the answer summation over $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$ and no average over questions.\n   259\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   260\t\tThe unitaries may be taken to be $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $V_{\\mathrm{B}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:v-swap-unitary}.\n   261\t\\end{lemma}\n   262\t\\begin{proof}\n   263\t\t\\uses{def:v-swap-unitary, lem:v-swap-conjugation, lem:tildew-product-form, lem:qld-construct-the-paulis, lem:qld-win-implications, fact:triangle-for-simeq, def:bracket, lem:cancellation, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree, lem:qld-4-7}\n   264\t\tFor the first item, the self-consistency of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j$ (Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}) is equivalent, since these observables are Hermitian involutions (Lemma~\\ref{lem:tildew-product-form}), to $\\E_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}} \\E_{\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M} \\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ot \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2$; setting $\\ket{\\vartheta} = V_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ and using the exact conjugation identities of Lemma~\\ref{lem:v-swap-conjugation}, this becomes $\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - \\delta_S/2$ for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, where $H_W = \\E_j \\E_{\\tilde{u}} \\, \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\bigl( \\E_{s \\in \\F_q} \\tau^W(s) \\ot \\tau^W(s) \\bigr)^{\\ot M}$ by the tensor factorization of $\\tau^W(\\tilde{u})$ and the invariance of the uniform average over $s$ under $s \\mapsto e_j s$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:cancellation}, $\\E_{s,s'} \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') \\ot \\tau^X(s)\\tau^Z(s') = \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ on each pair of qudits, whence $H_X H_Z = \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$. The bounds $\\| (\\Id - H_W)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq \\delta_S$ and $\\bra{\\vartheta} H_W^2 \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - 2\\delta_S$, combined through the triangle inequality $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq 2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ (see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}), give $\\bra{\\vartheta} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta} \\geq 1 - 4\\delta_S$.\n   265\t\t% Case distinction added: the source normalizes the projected vector\n   266\t\t% without ensuring that it is nonzero; see Remark rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip.\n   267\t\tIf $\\delta_S > 1/8$, then, after enlarging the universal constant in $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$, both items of the lemma hold with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ an arbitrary unit state, since their left-hand sides never exceed $4$; assume therefore $\\delta_S \\leq 1/8$. Then the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ has squared norm at least $1 - 4\\delta_S \\geq 1/2 > 0$, and $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ may be defined as its normalization; the overlap of $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ equals the norm of that vector and is at least $\\sqrt{1 - 4\\delta_S} \\geq 1 - 4\\delta_S$, which yields the first item with a bound of $8\\delta_S = O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$. For the second item, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} gives, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$, $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\eps \\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ and $\\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\simeq_\\eps M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id$, where $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} = \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = a} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h$; combining these with Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} by Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle-for-simeq}, using $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$, yields $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}} \\Id \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$. Writing $\\ket{\\Delta} = \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$, the quantity to be bounded for the $\\mathrm{A}$-side relation equals $2 - 2\\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\Delta}$, since $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} = (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$. Replacing $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ by the averaged coarse-graining $\\E_u \\sum_{h' : g_{h'}(u) = g_h(u)} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ costs at most $md/q$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree} (distinct $h \\neq h'$ have distinct encodings $g_h \\neq g_{h'}$, of total degree at most $md$), and by \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the resulting sum equals $\\E_u \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger \\ket{\\Delta}$. Exchanging $\\ket{\\Delta}$ for $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ costs $O(\\delta_S^{1/4})$ by the first item, and the resulting expression $\\E_u \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ is at least $1 - O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$ by the $\\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}}$ relation above. Altogether the distance is $O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$; the $\\mathrm{B}$-side relation is proved identically.\n   268\t\\end{proof}\n   269\t\n   270\t\\begin{remark}[Two steps in the source proof of the extraction lemma]\\label{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}\n   271\t\tIn the source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, the triangle inequality gives $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq \\| (\\Id - H_X)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| + \\| (\\Id - H_Z)\\ket{\\vartheta} \\| \\leq 2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$, so that $\\| H_X \\ket{\\vartheta} - H_Z \\ket{\\vartheta} \\|^2 \\leq 4\\delta_S$; the source instead asserts the bound $2\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ for the \\emph{squared} norm, which holds only when $\\sqrt{\\delta_S} \\leq 1/2$, and consequently carries the term $\\sqrt{\\delta_S}$ into an overlap bound of $1 - 2\\delta_S - \\sqrt{\\delta_S}$. The proof above uses the corrected bound $4\\delta_S$, which gives the overlap bound $1 - 4\\delta_S$ and leaves the final bound $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}} = O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ unchanged. The source proof also normalizes the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ without ensuring that it is nonzero; the case distinction on $\\delta_S$ in the proof above supplies this, the conclusions of the lemma being trivial when $\\delta_S$ exceeds a universal constant.\n   272\t\\end{remark}\n   273\t\n   274\t\\section{Proof of the soundness of the Pauli basis test}\n   275\t\n   276\tWe can now prove the soundness theorem for the Pauli basis test, stated as Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-test}.\n   277\t\n   278\t\\begin{proof}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/0001/cf4cfa6eb26ad593c4e130d033b54e6a455a1ae1/diff.patch:5243:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_127","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'lem:symmetric-strat|symmetric-strat' blueprint/src/chapter/ch1[1-6]_qpbt_*.tex references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex; nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '350,390p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '215,255p'\"","aggregated_output":"blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:53:\tThe symmetrization argument of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} below invokes Naimark dilation in the form of Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark} (stated and proved in Chapter~\\ref{chap:projective}): a bipartite state together with two sub-measurement families can be replaced, without changing any of the correlations $\\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^y_b \\ket{\\psi}$, by a product-extended state together with projective sub-measurements on the enlarged spaces. When the input families are complete measurements, the dilated projective sub-measurements can be completed by assigning the residual projector of each family to one fixed outcome; the resulting projective measurements reproduce the same correlations on the extended state, since the correlation-preservation identity forces the extended state to lie in the range of the total projector of each dilated family. This completed form, with genuinely projective measurements, is the one used in Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat}; for the proof the source refers to Theorem 5.1 of \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}, which is stated for complete measurements. The source follows its statement of the dilation theorem with a sentence announcing an ``orthogonalization lemma'' from the literature that is never actually stated there; nothing in this chapter depends on it.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:120:% the supremum defining val* need not be attained. See rem:symmetric-strat-limit.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:121:\\begin{lemma}[Symmetrization of strategies for symmetric games]\\label{lem:symmetric-strat}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:138:\\begin{remark}[Attainment in the symmetrization lemma]\\label{rem:symmetric-strat-limit}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:139:\tThe source states the conclusion of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} in the stronger form: there exists a symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\\eps$. Its proof, reproduced above, produces, for each $\\eps' > \\eps$, such a strategy of value at least $1-\\eps'$, and the stronger form does not follow: the dilated dimension $d$ may grow as $\\eps' \\downarrow \\eps$, and the supremum defining $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:tensor-product-value} need not be attained by any strategy. The statement above therefore records the approximate conclusion, which is what the proof establishes; the stronger form would require an additional attainment or compactness argument.\n   350\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-ccl}\n   351\t\t\tP_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\approx_{\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta)}\\, Q_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}}\\,.\n   352\t\t\\end{equation}\n   353\t\\end{lemma}\n   354\t\\begin{proof}\n   355\t\tThis lemma is imported: it first appears in~\\cite{KV11}, and a\n   356\t\tself-contained proof is given in the reference [ML20] cited\n   357\t\tin~\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. The proof is not reproduced here.\n   358\t\\end{proof}\n   359\t\n   360\t\\section{Strategies}\n   361\t\\label{sec:commutation}\n   362\t\n   363\t\\begin{definition}[The strategy under analysis]\n   364\t\t\\label{def:projective-strategy-setup}\n   365\t\t\\uses{def:admissible, def:pauli-question-distribution, def:pauli-win-predicate, def:tensor-product-strategy, def:tensor-product-value, def:projective-strategy-general, thm:naimark}\n   366\t\tFix an admissible parameter tuple $(q,m,d)$\n   367\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}) and a strategy\n   368\t\t$\\mathcal{S} = (\\ket{\\psi}, M)$ for the game\n   369\t\t$G^{\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ that succeeds with probability at least\n   370\t\t$1 - \\eps$, where $\\ket{\\psi}$ is a bipartite state on registers\n   371\t\t$\\mathsf{A}$ and $\\mathsf{B}$ with associated Hilbert spaces\n   372\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$. The symbol $M$ denotes\n   373\t\tthe measurement operators of both players; which player is meant is\n   374\t\tindicated by the register on which the operator acts (for example,\n   375\t\t$M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}}$ indicates that\n   376\t\t$M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ is viewed as an operator acting on register\n   377\t\t$\\mathsf{A}$). By Naimark's theorem (Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}), at the\n   378\t\tcost of extending $\\ket{\\psi}$ by sufficiently many ancilla qubits\n   379\t\tinitialized in the state $\\ket{0}$, all measurements in $\\mathcal{S}$\n   380\t\tmay be assumed projective; the extended state\n   381\t\t$(\\ket{\\psi} \\ot \\ket{0\\cdots 0} \\ot \\ket{0\\cdots 0})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}}$\n   382\t\tis again denoted $\\ket{\\psi}$. All statements below refer to this\n   383\t\tprojective strategy on the extended state.\n   384\t\\end{definition}\n   385\t\n   386\tThe strategy $\\mathcal{S}$ contains measurement operators for each of the\n   387\tquestion types and question contents of the Pauli basis test\n   388\t(Definition~\\ref{def:pauli-win-predicate}), such as\n   389\t$\\{M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ for all $u \\in \\F_q^m$.\n   390\t\n   215\t\t\t\t\\E_{\\omega \\, : \\, \\tr((r_\\xpt \\cdot\n   216\t                          u_\\xpt) \\cdot (r_\\zpt \\cdot u_\\zpt)) \\neq 0} \\Lambda_\\omega = 1 - O(\\eps)\\;.\n   217\t\t\t\\]\n   218\t\n   219\t\t\\item \\label{enu:qld-ms-cons} (\\textbf{Magic square consistency check}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over anticommuting $\\omega = (u_\\xpt,u_\\zpt,r_\\xpt,r_\\zpt)$,\n   220\t\t\\begin{gather}\n   221\t\t\tM^{(\\Point,X),u_\\xpt}_{[\\tr(\\cdot r_\\xpt) = a]} \\otimes \\Id_\\bob \\simeq_\\eps \\Id_\\alice \\otimes M^{\\Variable_1,\\omega}_{a} \\;,\\\\\n   222\t\t\tM^{(\\Point,Z),u_\\zpt}_{[\\tr(\\cdot r_\\zpt) = a]} \\otimes \\Id_\\bob \\simeq_\\eps \\Id_\\alice \\otimes M^{\\Variable_5,\\omega}_{a}\\;,\n   223\t\t\\end{gather}\t\n   224\t\twhere the answer summations are over $\\F_2$.\n   225\t\t\n   226\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   227\t\tFurthermore, all of the approximations above hold with ``$\\approx_\\eps$'' instead of ``$\\simeq_\\eps$'', and they also hold with the tensor factors interchanged.\n   228\t\\end{lemma}\n   229\t\n   230\t\\begin{proof}\n   231\t\tSince the question types are sampled according to the type graph $G^\\Pauli$ of\n   232\t  finite size in \\cref{fig:type-graph-pauli} in the game\n   233\t  $\\game^\\pauli_\\qldparams$, the probability of any of the subtests of\n   234\t  $\\decider^\\pauli_\\qldparams$ (described in \\cref{fig:decider_pauli})\n   235\t  being executed is at least some universal constant.\n   236\t  Thus the consistency conditions expressed in Items\n   237\t  \\ref{enu:qld-cons},~\\ref{enu:qld-ld},~\\ref{enu:qld-pauli-basis-cons} of the\n   238\t  lemma follow directly from the corresponding tests in\n   239\t  \\cref{fig:decider_pauli} (where \\Cref{enu:qld-ld} follows from the\n   240\t  consistency test that is a subtest of $\\decider^\\ld_\\ldparams$) and the fact\n   241\t  that the strategy $\\strategy$ is assumed to succeed with probability at least\n   242\t  $1 - \\eps$ in $\\game^\\pauli_\\qldparams$.\n   243\t\t\t\t\t\n   244\t  For the remaining items, recall that by Fact~\\ref{fact:omega-anticomm-prob}\n   245\t  the probability that a tuple $\\omega=(u_\\xpt,u_\\zpt,r_\\xpt,r_\\zpt)$ sampled as\n   246\t  question content for a question of type $\\Pair$, $(\\Pair,W)$, $\\Constraint_i$\n   247\t  or $\\Variable_j$ is commuting is at least $(1 - 3md/q) \\cdot (1/2)$, which is\n   248\t  at least $1/4$ assuming $6md \\leq q$. We claim this assumption holds\n   249\t  without loss of generality. Indeed, observe that the function\n   250\t  $\\delta$ in \\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix} is monotonically increasing in $a$, so without loss of generality we may assume that $a\n   251\t  \\geq 6$.  Then it follows that we may assume that $6md \\leq q$\n   252\t  without loss of generality, since otherwise, $\\delta \\geq a md /q\n   253\t  \\geq 1$ and the conclusion of the theorem holds trivially.\n   254\t  The same lower bound holds for the probability that $\\omega$ is anticommuting.\n   255\t  Therefore, the consistency conditions in\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
