/Users/dengnifer/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/0001/4277943db23d2dde26e90908ede8041fb7902e36/diff.patch:5466:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_119","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '748,904p'; nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex | sed -n '215,330p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '1080,1265p'\"","aggregated_output":"   748\t\tSimilarly, every single-party relation of the form\n   749\t\t$M_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\approx N_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}$ also\n   750\t\tholds on the register pairs $\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$,\n   751\t\t$\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{B}'$ and $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}''$. These variants\n   752\t\tare called the \\emph{symmetric equivalents} of the derived relation.\n   753\t\tSeveral lemmas below conclude by asserting that all symmetric\n   754\t\tequivalents also hold, and later chapters invoke exactly these\n   755\t\tvariants.\n   756\t\\end{definition}\n   757\t\n   758\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency and approximate commutation of the expanded point measurements]\n   759\t\t\\label{lem:qld-comm-cons}\n   760\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-point-trace-projection, def:expanded-state, def:approx-question-indexed-operators, def:povm-distance, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   761\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Anticom}}(\\eps) = \\poly(\\eps)$\n   762\t\tsuch that the following holds, with respect to the state\n   763\t\t$\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state}.\n   764\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   765\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on\n   766\t\t\taverage over $r \\in \\F_q$ and $u \\in \\F_q^m$ (the relation does not\n   767\t\t\tdepend on $r$), we have\n   768\t\t\t\\[\n   769\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   770\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_\\eps\\,\n   771\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   772\t\t\t\\]\n   773\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$.\n   774\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Approximate commutation}) For all $b, b' \\in \\F_2$,\n   775\t\t\ton average over uniformly random\n   776\t\t\t$\\omega = (u_X,u_Z,r_X,r_Z) \\in (\\F_q^m)^2 \\times (\\F_q)^2$, we\n   777\t\t\thave\n   778\t\t\t\\[\n   779\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),u_X,r_X}_{b}\\,\n   780\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),u_Z,r_Z}_{b'})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   781\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Anticom}}}\\,\n   782\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),u_Z,r_Z}_{b'}\\,\n   783\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),u_X,r_X}_{b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\\,.\n   784\t\t\t\\]\n   785\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   786\t\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also\n   787\t\thold, and the proof yields $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Anticom}}(\\eps) = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   788\t\\end{lemma}\n   789\t\\begin{proof}\n   790\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-win-implications, lem:qld-win-implications-obs, fact:data-processing, lem:twisted-commutation, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-point-trace-projection}\n   791\t\tFor item~1: the original point measurements\n   792\t\t$\\{M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a\\}$ are $\\eps$-self-consistent between\n   793\t\tregisters $\\mathsf{A}$ and $\\mathsf{B}$ in $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$\n   794\t\t(item~\\ref{enu:qld-cons} of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}), the\n   795\t\tPauli projections $\\{\\tau^{W,u}_a\\}$ are perfectly self-consistent\n   796\t\tbetween registers $\\mathsf{A}'$ and $\\mathsf{A}''$ on the EPR ancillas,\n   797\t\tand the data processing inequality (Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing})\n   798\t\tcombines the two through the convolution form of\n   799\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}. For item~2:\n   800\t\tby~\\eqref{eq:lc-22}, each side expands as\n   801\t\t$\\frac14 \\big( \\Id + (-1)^b \\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X) + (-1)^{b'} \\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z) + (-1)^{b+b'} \\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X) \\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z) \\big)$\n   802\t\trespectively its reverse-ordered analogue, so it suffices to commute\n   803\t\tthe two expanded observables. By~\\eqref{eq:lc-23},\n   804\t\t$\\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X)\\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z) = X^{r_X}(u_X) Z^{r_Z}(u_Z) \\ot \\tau^X(r_X \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u_X)) \\tau^Z(r_Z \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u_Z))$;\n   805\t\tthe strategy observables approximately commute or anticommute with sign\n   806\t\t$(-1)^{\\gamma(\\omega)}$ and error $\\sqrt{\\eps}$\n   807\t\t(\\eqref{eq:pts-obs-commutation} of\n   808\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications-obs}), while the generalized Pauli\n   809\t\tobservables exactly commute or anticommute with the same sign\n   810\t\t(see Lemma~\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation}). The two signs cancel, so\n   811\t\t$\\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X)\\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z) \\approx_{\\sqrt{\\eps}} \\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z)\\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X)$\n   812\t\ton average over uniformly random $\\omega$, giving the approximate\n   813\t\tcommutation with $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Anticom}} = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   814\t\\end{proof}\n   815\t\n   816\t\\begin{definition}[Expanded line measurements]\n   817\t\t\\label{def:expanded-line-measurement}\n   818\t\t% In the source this measurement is defined inside the proof of the lemma\n   819\t\t% labelled lem:qld-comm-line-cons (lines 552-556); it is recorded as a\n   820\t\t% standalone definition because Chapter chap:qpbt-combining uses the\n   821\t\t% concrete measurement, not merely its existence.\n   822\t\t\\uses{def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:expanded-observables, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:low-degree-encoding, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, def:line, def:pauli-win-predicate}\n   823\t\tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, a line $\\ell$, and $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$,\n   824\t\tdefine\n   825\t\t\\[\n   826\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\n   827\t\t\t\\,=\\, \\sum_{\\substack{f',f'' \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell) \\\\ f' + f'' = f}}\n   828\t\t\tM^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{f'} \\ot \\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}\\,,\n   829\t\t\\]\n   830\t\twhere the projector $\\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}$, acting on register\n   831\t\t$\\mathsf{A}'$ (or on $\\mathsf{A}''$, following the register conventions\n   832\t\tof Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-observables}), is the outcome-$f''$\n   833\t\tprojector of the following projective measurement: measure all $n$\n   834\t\tqudits in the basis $W$, obtaining an outcome $h \\in \\F_q^{n}$, and\n   835\t\treturn the polynomial $f''$ obtained by substituting the\n   836\t\tparameterization of $\\ell$ into the low-degree encoding\n   837\t\t$g_h \\colon \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$\n   838\t\t(see Definition~\\ref{def:low-degree-encoding}): writing $(u_0,v)$ for\n   839\t\tthe base point and direction of $\\ell$, the outcome $f''$ is\n   840\t\t$g_h(u_0 + tv)$, expanded as a polynomial in the variable $t$ from the\n   841\t\tcoefficient representation of $g_h$. Since $g_h$ has individual degree\n   842\t\tat most $d$, hence total degree at most $md$, the substituted\n   843\t\tpolynomial has degree at most $md$ in $t$, so\n   844\t\t$f'' \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$. The strategy measurement\n   845\t\t$\\{M^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{f'}\\}$ is indexed by the answers of the\n   846\t\tprescribed form (Definition~\\ref{def:pauli-win-predicate}): for a\n   847\t\tdiagonal line these are the elements of $\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$, and for an\n   848\t\taxis-parallel line the elements of $\\deg_d(\\ell)$, regarded as a\n   849\t\tmeasurement with outcomes in $\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$ through the inclusion\n   850\t\t$\\deg_d(\\ell) \\subseteq \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$ of\n   851\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:ideg-deg-polynomials}, with the zero operator\n   852\t\tassigned to outcomes outside $\\deg_d(\\ell)$. For an axis-parallel\n   853\t\t$\\ell$, with direction $e_i$, the substituted polynomial also has\n   854\t\tdegree at most $d$ in $t$ --- its degree is the individual degree of\n   855\t\t$g_h$ in the $i$-th variable --- so\n   856\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f = 0$ unless $f \\in \\deg_d(\\ell)$.\n   857\t\tIn all cases\n   858\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)}$ is a\n   859\t\tprojective measurement.\n   860\t\\end{definition}\n   861\t\n   862\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency of the expanded line measurements]\n   863\t\t\\label{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}\n   864\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:line-point-dist, def:approx-question-indexed-operators, def:povm-distance, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   865\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\poly(\\eps)$\n   866\t\tsuch that the following holds, with respect to the state\n   867\t\t$\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state}. For all\n   868\t\t$W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and lines $\\ell$ there exists a projective measurement\n   869\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)}$,\n   870\t\tacting on registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$ or\n   871\t\t$\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, such that:\n   872\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   873\t\t\t\\item \\label{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\n   874\t\t\t(\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and\n   875\t\t\t$r \\in \\F_q$ (the relation does not depend on $r$), on average over\n   876\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   877\t\t\tdistribution (Definition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist}),\n   878\t\t\t\\[\n   879\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   880\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   881\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   882\t\t\t\\]\n   883\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   884\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}\n   885\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements I}) On average over\n   886\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   887\t\t\tdistribution,\n   888\t\t\t\\[\n   889\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   890\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   891\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   892\t\t\t\t\\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f(u)})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   893\t\t\t\\]\n   894\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   895\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2}\n   896\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements II}) On average over\n   897\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   898\t\t\tdistribution,\n   899\t\t\t\\[\n   900\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   901\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   902\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   903\t\t\t\\]\n   904\t\t\twhere\n   215\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\n   216\t\t= \\E_{r,s} (-1)^{\\tr(ra+sb)} \\mathcal{L}^{x,z,r,s}$\n   217\t\tfor $a, b \\in \\F_q$: exact linearity makes each $\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ a\n   218\t\tprojection, and the family sums to the identity because\n   219\t\t$\\mathcal{L}^{x,z,0,0} = \\Id$, so $\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\}$ is a\n   220\t\tprojective measurement recovering $\\F_q$-valued outcomes from the binary\n   221\t\tdata by Fourier averaging over $(r,s)$. Its self-consistency\n   222\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-q-self-cons} follows from Lemma~\\ref{lem:avg-closeness}\n   223\t\ttogether with the self-consistency of the $\\{\\mathcal{O}^{\\omega}\\}$;\n   224\t\tthe consistency \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} follows from a Fourier\n   225\t\tcomputation that replaces $\\mathcal{L}$ by $\\mathcal{O}$, then by\n   226\t\t$\\hat{R}$, then by the ordered product of refined point measurements, and\n   227\t\tfinally resolves the averages over $r$ and $s$ using\n   228\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:cancellation}; and \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-xz} follows\n   229\t\tby inserting one application of the approximate commutation of\n   230\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons} into the same chain. Repeating the argument\n   231\t\twith the tensor factors on registers $\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{B}'$ and\n   232\t\t$\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}''$ gives all symmetric equivalents.\n   233\t\\end{proof}\n   234\t\n   235\t\\section{Combined line measurements}\n   236\t\n   237\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined line measurements]\n   238\t\t\\label{lem:qld-xz-lines}\n   239\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, def:line, def:line-point-dist, def:consistency,\n   240\t\t\tdef:bracket, def:expanded-state, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   241\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_P(\\eps,m,d,q) = \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$ such\n   242\t\tthat the following holds. For\n   243\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ and every pair of\n   244\t\tlines $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$ in $\\F_q^m$ there exists a POVM\n   245\t\t$\\{T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\}$ acting on $\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$,\n   246\t\twith answers consisting of pairs of polynomials\n   247\t\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$, such that on\n   248\t\taverage over pairs $(\\ell_X, x)$ and $(\\ell_Z, z)$ independently sampled\n   249\t\tfrom the line-point distribution\n   250\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist}),\n   251\t\t\\[\n   252\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}\n   253\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a,\\ \\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}\n   254\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   255\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_P}\n   256\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   257\t\t\\]\n   258\t\tas well as all symmetric equivalents of this approximation. Moreover, if\n   259\t\t$\\ell_W$ is axis-parallel, then $f_W \\in \\deg_{d}(\\ell_W)$.\n   260\t\\end{lemma}\n   261\t\\begin{proof}\n   262\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-line-measurement, lem:qld-4-10, fact:add-a-proj,\n   263\t\t\tlem:cool-closeness-fact, lem:qld-comm-line-cons, fact:agreement,\n   264\t\t\tfact:data-processing, lem:pasting, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}\n   265\t\tDefine, for all lines $\\ell_X, \\ell_Z$ and\n   266\t\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$, the sandwich\n   267\t\toperator\n   268\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\n   269\t\t= \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{f_X}\n   270\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},Z),\\ell_Z}_{f_Z}\n   271\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{f_X}$,\n   272\t\twhich forms a valid POVM; since the lines measurements come from a valid\n   273\t\tstrategy for the low-degree test, on an axis-parallel line $\\ell_W$ the\n   274\t\toutcome $f_W$ lies in $\\deg_d(\\ell_W)$, and this property is inherited by\n   275\t\t$T$. As intermediate consistency relations, write\n   276\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet} = \\sum_b \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ and\n   277\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{\\bullet,b} = \\sum_a \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ for the two\n   278\t\tmarginals of the combined point measurement of\n   279\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}. Applying\n   280\t\tLemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj} to \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} and then\n   281\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:cool-closeness-fact} gives, on average over uniform\n   282\t\t$x, z$,\n   283\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   284\t\t\\approx_\\delta\n   285\t\t\\sum_b (\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   286\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$,\n   287\t\tand a direct norm computation from this display and\n   288\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} yields the marginal consistencies\n   289\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   290\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$ and\n   291\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{\\bullet,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   292\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$.\n   293\t\tFrom items 1 and 3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}, converting\n   294\t\tbetween $\\approx$ and $\\simeq$ with Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} and\n   295\t\tsumming outcomes with Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, the lines\n   296\t\tmeasurements are consistent with the points measurements, and combining\n   297\t\twith the marginal consistencies gives, on average over the line-point\n   298\t\tdistribution,\n   299\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\simeq_{\\delta_Q}\n   300\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}\n   301\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a]})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$\n   302\t\ttogether with the analogous relation for $Z$. The conclusion now follows\n   303\t\tfrom the pasting lemma (Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}) applied with\n   304\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ in the role of the combined measurement,\n   305\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}$ and\n   306\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},Z),\\ell_Z}$ in the roles of the two factor\n   307\t\tmeasurements, $T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}$ in the role of the pasted measurement,\n   308\t\t$\\delta := \\delta_Q$, and $\\eta := md/q$, the last justified by the\n   309\t\tSchwartz--Zippel bound (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree})\n   310\t\tapplied to the polynomials $f_X, f_Z$ of total degree at most $md$; the\n   311\t\ttwo hypotheses of the pasting lemma are supplied by the preceding display\n   312\t\tand by \\eqref{eq:qld-q-self-cons}, and the distribution over\n   313\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$ is that of two independent uniformly random lines.\n   314\t\tThis gives the stated relation with $\\delta_P$ equal to the pasting\n   315\t\terror, a $\\poly(\\eps, md/q)$ function, and the identical argument with\n   316\t\tthe registers modified gives all symmetric equivalents.\n   317\t\\end{proof}\n   318\t\n   319\t\\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}\n   320\t\\label{sec:apply-ldt}\n   321\t\n   322\tThe combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next\n   323\tmerged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that\n   324\tencode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is\n   325\tdone by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking\n   326\tthe quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test\n   327\t(Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The instantiation at dimension $2m+2$\n   328\trequires that $2m+2$ divide $q$; the statements below carry this hypothesis\n   329\texplicitly, and Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} discusses its\n   330\trelationship to the admissibility of $(q,m,d)$.\n  1080\t            \\begin{enumerate}\n  1081\t              \\item If $1 \\leq j \\leq m$, then set $i_\\xpt = j$ and\n  1082\t                choose $i_\\zpt$ to be uniformly random in $\\{1, \\dots,\n  1083\t                m\\}$.\n  1084\t              \\item If  $m+1 \\leq j \\leq 2m$, then set $i_\\xpt$ to be\n  1085\t                uniformly random in $\\{1, \\dots, m\\}$ and $i_\\zpt = j - m$.\n  1086\t              \\item If $2m + 1 \\leq j \\leq 2m+2$, then set $i_\\xpt$\n  1087\t                and $i_\\zpt$ to both be uniformly random in $\\{1, \\dots, m\\}$.\n  1088\t              \\item Let $v'_\\xpt$ be the canonical representative\n  1089\t                $L_{e_{i_\\xpt}}^\\lnf(v_\\xpt)$, and\n  1090\t                likewise let $v'_\\zpt$ be the canonical\n  1091\t                representative $L_{e_{i_\\zpt}}^\\lnf(v_{\\zpt})$ (see\n  1092\t                \\Cref{def:line-representative} for the definition of\n  1093\t                the canonical representative of a line). Note that\n  1094\t                $v_\\xpt$ lies on the line $\\line_\\xpt = (v'_\\xpt,\n  1095\t                e_{i_\\xpt})$, and similarly for $v_\\zpt$ and\n  1096\t                $\\line_{\\zpt} = (v'_\\zpt, e_{i_\\zpt})$.\n  1097\t              \\end{enumerate}\n  1098\t              We claim that this construction satisfies Property~2 of the lemma. We show this first $(\\line_X,\\line_Z)$ and $u_X$, the other case being symmetric. If we are in case (a), then $u_X$ is a uniformly random point on the line $\\line_X=(v_X, e_j)$ and $\\line_Z=(v_Z,e_{i_Z})$, which is independent from $\\line_X$. Thus, the marginal distribution over $(\\line_X,u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ in this case is equal to $D_{\\ALine, j}$ and furthermore even conditioned on $(\\line_X,u_X)$, $\\line_Z$ is distributed according to $D_{\\ALine,i_Z}$ for a uniformly random $i_Z$. Cases (b) and (c) are analogous except that in this case even conditioned on $j$, $i_X$ is uniform. \n  1099\t      \\item Suppose $\\ell$ is a random diagonal line $(v, s, w)$,\n  1100\t        where $v = (v_\\xpt, v_\\zpt, \\alpha, \\beta)$, and\n  1101\t        where $w = (w_\\xpt, w_\\zpt, \\gamma, \\delta) $ is a direction vector whose first nonzero coordinate is\n  1102\t         $j = \\chi(s)$ (where $\\chi$ is defined in \\Cref{eq:chi-func}). We sample $\\line_\\xpt =  (v'_\\xpt, s_\\xpt,\n  1103\t        w'_\\xpt)$ and $\\line_\\zpt = (v'_\\zpt, s_\\zpt, w'_\\zpt)$ in the following\n  1104\t        manner:\n  1105\t        \\begin{enumerate}\n  1106\t          \\item If $1 \\leq j \\leq m$, then $w'_\\xpt = w_\\xpt$ and $w'_\\zpt = w_\\zpt$. We choose $s_\\xpt$ and $s_\\zpt$ such that $\\chi(s_\\xpt) = j$ and $\\chi(s_\\zpt) = 1$.\n  1107\t          \\item If $m+1 \\leq j \\leq 2m$, then $w'_\\xpt$ is a random\n  1108\t            diagonal line direction (and $s_{\\xpt}$ is chosen accordingly), and $w'_\\zpt = w_\\zpt$, with $s_\\zpt$ such that $\\chi(s_\\zpt) = j - m$.\n  1109\t          \\item If $2m+1 \\leq j \\leq 2m+2$ then both $w_\\xpt$, and\n  1110\t            $w_\\zpt$ are uniformly random, with $s_\\xpt$ and $s_\\zpt$ chosen accordingly.\n  1111\t          \\item Set $v'_\\xpt = L_{w'_{\\xpt}}^\\lnf(v_\\xpt)$ and $v'_\\zpt = L_{w'_{\\zpt}}^\\lnf(v_\\zpt)$.\n  1112\t          \\end{enumerate}\n  1113\t          We claim that this construction satisfies Property~2 of the Lemma. We show this first $(\\line_X,\\line_Z)$ and $u_X$, the other case being symmetric.  If we are in case (a), then $u_X$ is a uniformly random point on the diagonal line $(v_X, s_X, w_X)$ which is equal to $\\line_X$. Thus, the marginal distribution over $(\\line_X, u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ in this case is equal to $D_{\\DLine, j}$. Furthermore, conditioned on $(\\line_X,u_X)$, $\\line_Z$ is distributed according to $D_{\\DLine,1}$. The other cases can be analyed analogously, with the only difference being how the indices $i,j$ of $\\line_X$ and $\\line_Z$ distributions are chosen. \n  1114\t\t\t\t\t\t    \\end{enumerate}\n  1115\t\tLet $D$ be the distribution over tuples $(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z)$ induced by this procedure. This distribution $D$ satisfies Property 1 in the lemma by  construction, and Property 2 as argued above.\n  1116\t    \\end{proof}\n  1117\t\n  1118\t\\begin{proof}[Proof of \\Cref{lem:qld-4-13}]\n  1119\tWe construct the measurements $\\hat{Q}^\\line_f$\n  1120\tout of the lines measurements $T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}$\n  1121\tguaranteed by \\Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines}. To do so we first show how\n  1122\tto map any line-point pair $(\\line, u)$ in the expanded space\n  1123\t$\\F_q^{2m+2}$ to two pairs  $(\\line_X, u_X)$ and  $(\\line_Z, u_Z)$ in $\\F_q^m$. We will \n  1124\tdefine $\\hat{Q}^\\line$ in terms of $T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}$ and\n  1125\tshow that the resulting measurement is consistent with the combined\n  1126\tpoints measurement $\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha, \\beta}$ from \\Cref{lem:qld-4-12}.\n  1127\t\n  1128\tTo define $\\hat{Q}^\\line$ we make use of the distribution $D$ from \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}. Let $D_{|\\line}$ denote the distribution derived from $D$ by conditioning on the first element being $\\line$. Then for every line $\\line$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and function $f \\in \\deg_{(md+1)}(\\line)$, define\n  1129\t\\[ \\hat{Q}^\\line_f = \\E_{(\\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D_{|\\line}} \\sum_{\\substack{f_X \\in \\deg_{md}(\\line_X), f_Z \\in \\deg_{md}(\\line_Z):\\\\ f = \\combine_{f_X, f_Z}}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}\\;, \\]\n  1130\twhere $\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ is defined in~\\eqref{eq:combine-lines}. Note that $\\{\\hat{Q}^\\line_f\\}$ forms a valid POVM for every choice of line $\\line$, and moreover that $\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ is well-defined for the lines $\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z$ because of Property 2 of \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}. \n  1131\t\n  1132\tObserve that by Property~3 of \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}, whenever $\\line$ is axis-parallel, $\\line_X$ and $\\line_Z$ are axis-parallel also. Thus, in this case, by \\Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines} it holds that the outcomes $f_X$ and $f_Z$ both have degree $d$. By definition of $\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ it holds that the outcome $f$ of $\\{\\hat{Q}^\\line_f\\}$ has degree $d$ as well.\n  1133\t\n  1134\tWe argue the following bound:\n  1135\t\\begin{align}\n  1136\t  \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} &\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot (I - \\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\,=\\, O\\big( m \\cdot (\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} + \\delta_{\\Line}^{1/2})\\big)\\;. \\label{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}\n  1137\t\\end{align}\n  1138\tThis bound will follow from chaining the following three claims. \n  1139\t\n  1140\t\\begin{claim}\\label{claim:17-1}\n  1141\t\\begin{align}\n  1142\t  \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} &\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot \\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\notag\\\\\n  1143\t       &\\approx_{\\delta_Q^{1/2}} \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D}  \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line}  \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)}  \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\;. \n  1144\t\\end{align}\n  1145\t\\end{claim}\n  1146\t\n  1147\t\\begin{proof}\n  1148\tForm the difference and apply the Cauchy Schwarz inequality to obtain\n  1149\t\\begin{align*}\n  1150\t&\\Big|  \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z}\\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot \\big(\\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)} \\big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\Big|\\\\\n  1151\t&\\leq \\Big( \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\big\\|\\sqrt{T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} }\\ot \\Id \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\big\\|^2 \\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n  1152\t&\\qquad\\cdot \\Big( \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z}\\big\\| \\sqrt{T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}} \\ot \\big(\\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)} \\big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\big\\|^2 \\Big)^{1/2}\\;.\n  1153\t\\end{align*}\n  1154\tThe first term above is at most $1$. For the second term, writing \n  1155\t\\[W^{x,z}_{a,b} = \\big(\\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{a,b}- \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b}\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a}\\big)\\]\n  1156\tfor short\n  1157\twe bound it as \n  1158\t\\begin{align*}\n  1159\t\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line}  \\sum_{f_X, f_Z}\\big\\| \\sqrt{T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}} \\ot W^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\big\\|^2 \n  1160\t&\\leq \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{a,b}\\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Id \\ot (W^{x,z}_{a,b})^2 \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1161\t&= \\E_{(x,z)} \\sum_{a,b}\\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Id \\ot (W^{x,z}_{a,b})^2 \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1162\t&\\leq \\delta_Q\\;.\n  1163\t\\end{align*}\n  1164\tHere the first inequality uses that for any $x,z,a,b$, $\\sum_{f_X,f_X: f_X(x)=a,f_Z(z)=b} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}\\leq \\Id$. \n  1165\tIn the equality in the before-last line, the expectation is over independent uniform $x,z\\in \\F_q^m$. The equality holds because for a line $\\ell$ sampled from the line-point distribution $D$, a uniformly random point $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ on $\\ell$ is distributed as a uniformly random point in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$. The last inequality is by~\\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx}.\n  1166\t\\end{proof}\n  1167\t\n  1168\t\\begin{claim}\\label{claim:17-2}\n  1169\t\\begin{align}\n  1170\t  \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} &\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}  \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\notag\\\\\n  1171\t       &\\approx_{m\\delta_\\Line^{1/2}} \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D}  \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot  \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),Z}_{f_Z(z)} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\;. \n  1172\t\\end{align}\n  1173\t\\end{claim}\n  1174\t\n  1175\t\\begin{proof}\n  1176\tWe form the difference and apply the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality\n  1177\t\\begin{align*}\n  1178\t \\Big|\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} &\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} (\\Id-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)} ) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\Big|\\\\\n  1179\t&\\leq \\Big(\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\big\\| \\sqrt{ T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}} \\ot  \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\big\\|^2 \\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n  1180\t&\\qquad\\cdot \\Big(\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\big\\| \\sqrt{ T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}} \\ot  (\\Id-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\big\\|^2 \\Big)^{1/2}\\;.\n  1181\t\\end{align*}\n  1182\tThe first term above is at most $1$. The second term can be bounded as\n  1183\t\\begin{align}\n  1184\t\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} &\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\big\\| \\sqrt{ T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}} \\ot  (\\Id-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\big\\|^2 \\notag\\\\\n  1185\t&= \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}  T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z,x}_{a} \\ot  (\\Id-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a})^2 \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\notag\\\\\n  1186\t&= 1-\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}  T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z,x}_{a} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\;,\\label{eq:17-4}\n  1187\t\\end{align}\n  1188\twhere we used the shorthand $T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z,x}_{a} = \\sum_{f_X,f_Z: f_X(x)=a} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}$ and the second equality uses that $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a}\\}$ is a projective measurement. Using the definition \n  1189\t\\[\tT^{\\line_\\xpt,\\line_\\zpt}_{f_\\xpt,f_\\zpt} = \\hat{M}^{(\\Line,\\xpt),\\line_\\xpt}_{f_\\xpt} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Line,\\zpt),\\line_\\zpt}_{f_\\zpt}  \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Line,\\xpt),\\line_\\xpt}_{f_\\xpt} \\]\n  1190\tand the fact that $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\Line,\\xpt),\\line_\\xpt}_{f_\\xpt} \\}$ is projective we get\n  1191\t\\begin{align}\n  1192\t1-\\eqref{eq:17-4}&=\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\hat{M}^{(\\Line,\\xpt),\\line_\\xpt}_{[\\eval_x(\\cdot)=a]} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\;.\\label{eq:a17-8}\n  1193\t\\end{align}\n  1194\tBy Property~2 of \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines} the right-hand side of~\\eqref{eq:a17-8}\n  1195\t is a mixture of terms of the form\n  1196\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:a17-9}\n  1197\t \\E_{(\\line_X, x) \\sim D_{\\tvar, i}}  \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\hat{M}^{(\\Line,\\xpt),\\line_\\xpt}_{[\\eval_x(\\cdot)=a]} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\;, \n  1198\t\\end{equation}\n  1199\tfor $\\tvar \\in \\{\\ALine, \\DLine\\}$ and $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$. \n  1200\tHence by the discussion following Definition~\\ref{def:ith-restricted-line} and  item 3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons} it follows that\n  1201\t\\[ \\eqref{eq:a17-9} \\,=\\, O(m^2 \\delta_\\Line)\\;. \\]\n  1202\t\\end{proof}\n  1203\t\n  1204\t\\begin{claim}\\label{claim:17-3}\n  1205\t\\begin{align}\n  1206\t  \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D}\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line}\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot  \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \n  1207\t       &\\approx_{\\sqrt{m}\\cdot(\\delta_P^{1/4}+\\delta_Q^{1/4}+\\eps^{1/4})}1 \\;. \n  1208\t\\end{align}\n  1209\t\\end{claim}\n  1210\t\n  1211\t\\begin{proof}\n  1212\tAt first we proceed similarly to the proof of Claim~\\ref{claim:17-2}, bounding the difference using Cauchy-Schwarz as\n  1213\t\\begin{align*}\n  1214\t&\\Big| \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot  (\\Id-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\Big|\\\\\n  1215\t&\\leq\\Big(\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\big\\| \\sqrt{ T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}} \\ot  \\Id \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\big\\|^2 \\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n  1216\t&\\qquad\\cdot \\Big(\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\big\\| \\sqrt{ T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}} \\ot  (\\Id-\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\big\\|^2 \\Big)^{1/2}\\;.\n  1217\t\\end{align*}\n  1218\tThe first term is at most $1$. The second, as in the proof of Claim~\\ref{claim:17-2}, equals\n  1219\t\\begin{align}\n  1220\t1-\\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} &\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} \\sum_{b} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}  T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z, z}_{b} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\notag\\\\\n  1221\t&=1-\\E_{(\\line_X,\\line_Z)\\sim D_{\\tvar,i}\\times D_{\\tvar,j}}\n  1222\t\\E_{z\\in \\line_Z } \\sum_{b} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}  T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z, z}_{b}  \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\label{eq:a17-5}\\\\\n  1223\t&=1-\\E_{(\\line_X,\\line_Z)\\sim D_{\\tvar,i}\\times D_{\\tvar,j}}\n  1224\t\\E_{z\\in \\line_Z }\\E_{x\\in \\line_X} \\sum_{a,b} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}  T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{[\\eval_x(\\cdot)=a,\\eval_z(\\cdot)=b]}  \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\label{eq:a17-6}\n  1225\t\\end{align}\n  1226\twhere we use the shorthand $T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z, z}_{b} = \\sum_{f_X} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X,[\\eval_z(\\cdot)=b]}$ and $D_{\\tvar,i}\\times D_{\\tvar,j}$ to denote a mixture over all such distributions where $\\tvar\\in\\{\\ALine,\\DLine\\}$ and $i,j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$. Equality holds in~\\eqref{eq:a17-5} because by item 2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} for $(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z)\\sim D$ the marginal distribution on $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z)$ is a mixture of the product distributions  $D_{\\ALine, i}\\times D_{\\ALine, j}$ and $D_{\\DLine, i}\\times D_{\\DLine,j}$, for $i,j\\in \\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$. Moreover, conditioned on $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z)$, $z$ is a uniformly random point on $\\ell_X$. The last equality in~\\eqref{eq:a17-6} holds by definition of \n  1227\t\\begin{align*}\n  1228\tT^{\\line_X, \\line_Z, z}_{b}&= \\sum_{f_X} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X,[\\eval_z(\\cdot)=b]}\\\\\n  1229\t&= \\Es{x \\in \\line_X} \\sum_a \\sum_{f_X: f_X(x)=a} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X,[\\eval_z(\\cdot)=b]}\\\\\n  1230\t&= \\Es{x \\in \\line_X} \\sum_a  T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{[\\eval_x(\\cdot)=a,\\eval_z(\\cdot)=b]}\\;.\n  1231\t\\end{align*}\n  1232\tWe next write\n  1233\t\\begin{align*}\n  1234\t1-\\eqref{eq:a17-6}&\\approx_{m(\\sqrt{\\delta_P}+\\sqrt{\\delta_Q})}\\E_{(\\line_X,\\line_Z)\\sim D_{\\tvar,i}\\times D_{\\tvar,j}}\\E_{x\\in \\line_X} \\E_{z\\in \\line_Z } \\sum_{a,b} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a}\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b}  \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1235\t&= \\sum_{b} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b}  \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1236\t&\\geq 1-\\sqrt{\\eps}\\;,\n  1237\t\\end{align*}\n  1238\twhere the first approximation follows from the discussion following Definition~\\ref{def:ith-restricted-line} applied to the bounds from Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}, the second line uses that $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a}\\}$ is a measurement, and the last is by self-consistency of the points measurements (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}).\n  1239\t\\end{proof}\n  1240\t\n  1241\tTogether, Claim~\\ref{claim:17-1}, Claim~\\ref{claim:17-2} and Claim~\\ref{claim:17-3} show~\\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}. To complete the proof of~\\eqref{eq:qld-4-13}, observe that by Property~2 of \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines} the right-hand side of \\Cref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency} is a mixture of terms of the form\n  1242\tTogether, Claim~\\ref{claim:17-1}, Claim~\\ref{claim:17-2} and Claim~\\ref{claim:17-3} show {eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}. To complete the proof of~\\eqref{eq:qld-4-13}, observe that by Property~2 of \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines} the right-hand side of \\Cref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency} is a mixture of terms of the form\n  1243\t\\[ \\E_{(\\line_X, x) \\sim D_{\\tvar_1, i}} \\E_{(\\line_Z, z) \\sim D_{\\tvar_2, j}} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ot (I - (\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x), f_Z(z)})) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\]\n  1244\tfor $\\tvar_1, \\tvar_2 \\in \\{\\ALine, \\DLine\\}$ and $i, j \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$. Hence using~\\Cref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} it follows that\n  1245\t\\[ \\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency} \\,=\\, O(m^2 \\delta_P)\\;. \\]\n  1246\tThis establishes the first relation~\\eqref{eq:qld-4-13}. An analogous argument shows the second relation.\n  1247\t\n  1248\t\n  1249\t\n  1250\t\n  1251\t\n  1252\t\n  1253\t\n  1254\t\n  1255\t\n  1256\t\n  1257\t\n  1258\t\n  1259\t\n  1260\t\n  1261\t\n  1262\t\n  1263\t\t\n  1264\t\\end{proof}\n  1265\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
