ter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:690:\t$(\\C^2 \\ot \\C^2)^{\\ot Lk}$). Moreover, for all $W \\in \\{X, Z\\}$ and all\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:249:\tso the bottom-right cell is Hermitian, and it squares to the identity; hence every cell is a genuine $\\pm 1$-valued observable. The three observables in each row and each column commute pairwise --- for the third row and third column this again uses Equation~\\eqref{eq:am-i-hermitian} --- so the $\\mathsf{Constraint}$ measurements are well defined, and the strategy is symmetric, projective, and commuting. A two-outcome projective measurement is consistent on a state if and only if its observable $\\mathcal O$ satisfies $\\mathcal O \\ot I \\ket{\\psi} = I \\ot \\mathcal O \\ket{\\psi}$; since $A$ and $B$ are consistent on $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot n}$ by hypothesis and $\\sigma^X$, $\\sigma^Z$ are consistent on $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$, each cell observable is a product of consistent, commuting factors and is therefore consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$, and consistency of the $\\mathsf{Constraint}$ measurements follows from the cellwise consistencies together with commutativity. Finally, the observables in each row and each of the first two columns multiply to $I$, while those of the third column multiply to $-I$; for the measurement $\\{S_0, S_1\\}$ obtained by measuring a $\\mathsf{Constraint}_i$ triple and outputting the sum of the three bits, the observable $S_0 - S_1$ equals the product of the three cell observables, hence $\\pm I$, which forces the three answer bits to sum to $0$ (respectively, $1$ for $\\mathsf{Constraint}_6$) with certainty. Together with consistency this shows that every check of the Magic Square game is passed with probability $1$, so the strategy has value $1$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:276:\t% Admissibility of the full tuple is required here, not only the divisibility m | q:\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:450:\tFor all integers $R \\ge 4$, the parameter tuple $\\mathsf{introparams}(R) = (q,m,d)$ is admissible (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}) and satisfies $2^m \\ge R$. Furthermore, there exist universal constants $a' \\ge 1$ and $0 < b' \\le 1$ such that the function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps, m, d, q)$ of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} is at most\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:101:\tthe latter with respect to $\\ket{\\psi'}$. Furthermore,\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:157:\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also hold.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:258:\tas well as all symmetric equivalents of this approximation. Moreover, if\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:402:\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also hold.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:453:\twhere the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$. Moreover, if $\\ell$ is\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:558:\t\t$u_Z \\in \\ell_Z$. Moreover, the joint distribution of\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:769:\tfor every $x, z, a, b$. For the second factor, note that when $\\ell$ is\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:991:\tthe first route. Neither route, moreover, delivers the error form\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:93:Unless specified otherwise, all strategies considered in what follows are tensor product strategies, and we simply call them \\emph{strategies}; similarly, we refer to $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$ as the \\emph{value} of the game $\\mathfrak G$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:157:\tWhen the state $\\ket{\\psi}$ is clear from context we simply say that $\\{M_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ is consistent.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:175:\tWe say that a strategy $\\mathcal S$ for a game $\\mathfrak G$ is \\emph{PCC} if it is projective, consistent, and commuting for $\\mathfrak G$. Additionally, we say that a PCC strategy $\\mathcal S$ is \\emph{SPCC} if it is furthermore symmetric.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:443:and $V_{\\le k}$, $V_{\\ge k}$ are understood to be $V_{<k+1}$ and $V_{>k-1}$, respectively. For a register subspace $V'$ of $V$, the symbol $x^{V'}$ denotes the projection of $x$ onto $V'$ parallel to the complementary register subspace (Definition~\\ref{def:complementary}). Moreover, if $F \\colon V' \\to V'$ is a function and $x \\in V$, we write $x^F$ for $F(x^{V'})$; for example, in the following definition $x^{L_1}$ is shorthand for $L_1(x^{V_1})$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:457:We abbreviate ``conditionally linear'' as \\emph{CL}. Operationally, an $\\ell$-level CL function first applies $L_1$ to $x^{V_1}$; conditioned on the value $x^{L_1}$ it selects a register subspace $V_2$ and a linear map $L_2$ and applies $L_2$ to $x^{V_2}$; and so on, finally outputting $\\sum_{k=1}^{\\ell} x^{L_k}$. Every $1$-level CL function is linear, and conversely every linear map on $V$ is a $1$-level CL function. Moreover, for every $\\ell \\ge 1$ the collection of $\\ell$-level CL functions contains the collection of $(\\ell-1)$-level CL functions.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:464:Note that both players' questions are generated from the \\emph{same} uniformly random seed $x$; this shared-seed structure is what the introspection technique exploits. The following lemma elucidates the structural properties of $\\ell$-level CL functions. Recall that $x^{L_{<k}}$ and $x^{L_k}$ denote $L_{<k}(x)$ and $L_{k,\\,u}(x^{V_{k,\\,u}})$ where $u = L_{<k}(x)$.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
